o MongoDB, Elasticsearch, ClickHouse, Apache Iceberg, Quickwit, S3 and more. Built-in data transforms and Prometheus metrics."},"29":{"id":"/docs-27","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"MCP Server - Model Context Protocol server exposing 40+ tools for LLM integration. Works with Claude Desktop via stdio and HTTP transports."},"30":{"id":"/docs-28","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"Web UI - SvelteKit dashboard for stream/topic management, message browsing with JSON/string/XML decoders, and user management."},"31":{"id":"/docs-29","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"CLI - full-featured command-line interface with named connection contexts, session-based login, and shell completions."},"32":{"id":"/docs-30","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"SDKs - client libraries for 8 languages (Rust, Python, Java, Go, Node.js, C#, C++, PHP), most with runnable examples."},"33":{"id":"/docs-31","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"Benchmarking - built-in iggy-bench tool with a Yew/WebAssembly dashboard for performance testing."},"34":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"page","content":"Troubleshooting","breadcrumbs":["Documentation"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting"},"35":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-0","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting","content":"What the most common first-run failures look like, what causes them, and how to fix them."},"36":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-1","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"Client hangs with no output"},"37":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-2","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#docker-container-cant-be-reached","content":"Docker container can't be reached"},"38":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-3","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"Server exits with an io_uring error in a container"},"39":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-4","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#kernel-too-old","content":"Kernel too old"},"40":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-5","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#lost-the-root-password","content":"Lost the root password"},"41":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-6","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"\"Already exists\" error when running the CLI again"},"42":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-7","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-connects-then-hangs-at-login","content":"Client connects, then hangs at login"},"43":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-8","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-wont-start-on-docker-desktop","content":"Server won't start on Docker Desktop"},"44":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-9","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting","content":"This page covers the problems people most often hit the first time they run Apache Iggy. Each entry shows what you see, what causes it, and how to fix it."},"45":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-10","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting","content":"To collect diagnostic data for a bug report instead, see the snapshot command in CLI commands."},"46":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-11","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"What you see: the client starts and then prints nothing. It doesn't fail and it doesn't exit."},"47":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-12","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"Cause: nothing is listening on the address the client connects to, usually 127.0.0.1:8090. The server isn't running, is bound to a different address, or hasn't finished starting. By default the SDKs retry the connection forever, and the retries are only logged if your application has logging set up, so all you see is silence."},"48":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-13","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"Fix: check the server has started before starting the client. It logs a server client listeners started line that includes the address, for example tcp=127.0.0.1:8090. You can also check the port directly:"},"49":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-14","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"To make the client fail with an error instead of retrying forever, set reconnection_retries=0 on the connection string, or call .with_reconnection_max_retries(Some(0)) on the Rust client builder. Turning on logging in your application also shows each reconnect attempt."},"50":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-15","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#docker-container-cant-be-reached","content":"What you see: the server is running in Docker with -p 8090:8090, but clients on the host can't connect. On Docker Desktop the connection may be accepted and then reset straight away."},"51":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-16","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#docker-container-cant-be-reached","content":"Cause: the image doesn't override the server's addresses, so it binds 127.0.0.1 inside the container, where the published port can't reach it."},"52":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-17","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#docker-container-cant-be-reached","content":"Fix: set IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8090, and the equivalent for any other transport you expose, along with IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS. See the docker run and Compose examples in Docker & Helm."},"53":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-18","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"What you see: the server exits at startup with one of these:"},"54":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-19","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"On a machine with more cores, the same problems can show up as failed to create io_uring runtime for shard N instead."},"55":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-20","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"Cause: Docker's default seccomp profile blocks the io_uring system calls the server needs, or the container's locked-memory limit is too small for the server's io_uring rings."},"56":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-21","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"Fix: run the container with --security-opt seccomp=unconfined and --ulimit memlock=-1:-1, or with a custom seccomp profile and a locked-memory limit big enough for the server. See Why these capabilities? and Linux tuning."},"57":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-22","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#kernel-too-old","content":"What you see: the server exits with an io_uring Invalid Argument (EINVAL) report that includes a line like:"},"58":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-23","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#kernel-too-old","content":"Cause: on Linux the server needs kernel 5.19 or newer for the io_uring features it uses. WSL2 kernels can be missing io_uring features even on a newer version."},"59":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-24","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#kernel-too-old","content":"Fix: upgrade the host kernel to 5.19 or newer. Under WSL2, run wsl --update. Containers use the host's kernel, so it's the host that needs upgrading. See System requirements."},"60":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-25","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#lost-the-root-password","content":"What you see: you started the server without setting credentials, and you don't know the root password."},"61":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-26","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#lost-the-root-password","content":"Cause: if IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD aren't set and --with-default-root-credentials isn't used, the server generates a random root password on first start and logs it once:"},"62":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-27","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#lost-the-root-password","content":"It isn't logged again on later starts, because the credentials are only created when the data directory is new."},"63":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-28","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#lost-the-root-password","content":"Fix: set IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD before the first start, or use --with-default-root-credentials for development. If the password is already lost, deleting the data directory resets it, but that also deletes all data, so only do it on a development instance."},"64":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-29","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"What you see: running iggy stream create dev a second time fails:"},"65":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-30","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"The name is missing from the message because of a known bug where error details are lost between the server and the client (#3735)."},"66":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-31","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"Cause: the stream was created the first time. The CLI reports the server's error rather than skipping streams that already exist."},"67":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-32","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"Fix: nothing needs fixing. The stream exists and can be used as it is. Check with iggy stream list before creating it if you're running a setup script more than once."},"68":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-33","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-connects-then-hangs-at-login","content":"What you see: the client connects, then hangs with no error, while the server logs the connection being accepted and then dropped."},"69":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-34","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-connects-then-hangs-at-login","content":"Cause: the SDK and the server are from different releases. Clients from before 0.9.0 can't talk to a 0.9.0 server, and the login waits forever instead of failing (#4262)."},"70":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-35","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-connects-then-hangs-at-login","content":"Fix: use the SDK version that matches your server. For the 0.9.0 server that's the iggy crate 0.11.0 for Rust and apache-iggy 0.9.0 for Python. See Server compatibility for every SDK."},"71":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-36","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-wont-start-on-docker-desktop","content":"What you see: with 0.9.0 on Docker Desktop, every shard logs Failed to bind memory and the server exits with failed to bind shard 0 memory to its NUMA node."},"72":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-37","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-wont-start-on-docker-desktop","content":"Cause: the server binds each shard's memory to a NUMA node, and Docker Desktop's Linux VM rejects that. The same happens on WSL2 and other hosts with a single NUMA node."},"73":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-38","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-wont-start-on-docker-desktop","content":"Fix: add -e IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all to the docker run command. Each shard is still pinned to its own core; only the memory binding is skipped, and on a single NUMA node it does nothing useful. This is fixed in the next release."},"74":{"id":"/docs/ai/introduction","page_id":"/docs/ai/introduction","type":"page","content":"Introduction","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","AI"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/introduction"},"75":{"id":"/docs/ai/introduction-0","page_id":"/docs/ai/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/introduction","content":"The AI-related components of Apache Iggy: the MCP server that lets LLM applications work with a running Iggy server."},"76":{"id":"/docs/ai/introduction-1","page_id":"/docs/ai/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/introduction","content":"Apache Iggy includes an MCP server, so LLM applications can work with a running Iggy server. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open protocol that standardizes how applications provide context to LLMs."},"77":{"id":"/docs/ai/introduction-2","page_id":"/docs/ai/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/introduction","content":"Through the MCP server, an MCP client can check server health and statistics, create and manage streams, topics and partitions, send and poll messages, manage consumer groups and offsets, and administer users and personal access tokens. It connects to Iggy over TCP with its own credentials, and supports HTTP (the default) and stdio transports, with stdio suited to clients running on the same machine."},"78":{"id":"/docs/ai/introduction-3","page_id":"/docs/ai/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/introduction","content":"Access is limited in two layers. The [permissions] section of the MCP server's configuration can restrict it to read-only operations, and the Iggy user it connects as keeps its own permissions on the server. For production use, connect as a dedicated user with the minimum permissions it needs."},"79":{"id":"/docs/ai/introduction-4","page_id":"/docs/ai/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/introduction","content":"See MCP Server for how to run and configure it, the full list of tools, and client setup."},"80":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"page","content":"MCP Server","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","AI"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp"},"81":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-0","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp","content":"Run and configure the Apache Iggy MCP server, including transport, Iggy credentials and TLS."},"82":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-1","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#getting-started","content":"Getting started"},"83":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-2","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"Configuration"},"84":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-3","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#available-tools","content":"Available tools"},"85":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-4","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Server"},"86":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-5","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#streams","content":"Streams"},"87":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-6","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#topics","content":"Topics"},"88":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-7","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#partitions--segments","content":"Partitions & Segments"},"89":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-8","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#messages","content":"Messages"},"90":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-9","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer Groups"},"91":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-10","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#consumer-offsets","content":"Consumer Offsets"},"92":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-11","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#users--access-tokens","content":"Users & Access Tokens"},"93":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-12","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#permissions","content":"Permissions"},"94":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-13","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#claude-desktop-integration","content":"Claude Desktop integration"},"95":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-14","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#docker","content":"Docker"},"96":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-15","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#systemd-integration","content":"Systemd integration"},"97":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-16","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp","content":"The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open protocol that standardizes how applications provide context to LLMs. The Apache Iggy MCP Server is an implementation of the MCP protocol for the message streaming infrastructure. It is built using the rmcp crate and supports both stdio and HTTP transports."},"98":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-17","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#getting-started","content":"Start an Iggy 0.9.0 or edge broker using the getting started guide. From the matching apache/iggy checkout, run:"},"99":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-18","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#getting-started","content":"These are the development credentials configured in that guide. Use your broker's credentials or a PAT when connecting to an existing installation."},"100":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-19","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#getting-started","content":"The docker image is available, and can be fetched via docker pull apache/iggy-mcp:0.5.0."},"101":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-20","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"The minimal viable configuration requires at least the Iggy credentials, used to create the TCP connection to the running Iggy server that the MCP server talks to. You can choose between HTTP (the default) and STDIO transports (e.g. for the local usage with tools such as Claude Desktop choose stdio)."},"102":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-21","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"Set telemetry.enabled = true to export to a running collector. For HTTP export, set transport = \"http\" and use complete signal URLs: http://localhost:4318/v1/logs for logs and http://localhost:4318/v1/traces for traces. The MCP server does not append those paths."},"103":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-22","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"The configuration file must be in the toml format. By default, the server looks for core/ai/mcp/config.toml relative to its working directory. Set IGGY_MCP_CONFIG_PATH to use another path. Embedded defaults are loaded first, then the file if it exists, then environment overrides."},"104":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-23","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"Each setting can also be overridden using IGGY_MCP_<SECTION>_<KEY>, for example IGGY_MCP_IGGY_USERNAME or IGGY_MCP_HTTP_ADDRESS. Nested settings use the same underscore convention, such as IGGY_MCP_IGGY_TLS_ENABLED. Set IGGY_MCP_ENV_PATH to load a particular dotenv file; otherwise .env is searched for in the current directory and its parents. Existing environment variables take precedence over dotenv values."},"105":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-24","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"The token value can be either a literal PAT or a file: reference such as token = \"file:/run/secrets/iggy_pat\", in which case the token is read from the given file and surrounding whitespace is removed (~/ is expanded to the home directory). A non-empty token takes precedence over the username and password."},"106":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-25","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#available-tools","content":"The MCP server exposes these 41 tools:"},"107":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-26","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Tool"},"108":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-27","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Description"},"109":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-28","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"ping"},"110":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-29","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Health check"},"111":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-30","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"get_stats"},"112":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-31","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Server statistics (streams, topics, partitions, OS info)"},"113":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-32","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"get_me"},"114":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-33","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Current client info"},"115":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-34","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"get_client"},"116":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-35","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Get specific client details"},"117":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-36","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"get_clients"},"118":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-37","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"List all connected clients"},"119":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-38","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"get_cluster_metadata"},"120":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-39","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Cluster information"},"121":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-40","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"snapshot"},"122":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-41","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Collect troubleshooting data"},"123":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-42","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#streams","content":"Tool"},"124":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-43","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#streams","content":"Description"},"125":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-44","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#streams","content":"get_streams"},"126":{"id":"