ges requires update as well as read when auto_commit is true or the next strategy is used. The next strategy enables auto-commit automatically. Storing an offset requires update; deleting an offset requires delete. PAT creation and deletion require create and delete, respectively."},"207":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-126","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#permissions","content":"On top of this, the Iggy user account used by the MCP server has its own granular permissions. For production use, create a dedicated user with the minimum required permissions."},"208":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-127","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#claude-desktop-integration","content":"Set command to the absolute path of the built iggy-mcp executable. This Claude Desktop example uses the broker and development credentials from the getting started guide:"},"209":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-128","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#docker","content":"Create a shared network and start a development broker that advertises its container name:"},"210":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-129","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#docker","content":"After the broker is ready, run the MCP server on the same network:"},"211":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-130","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#docker","content":"The HTTP endpoint uses the configured Iggy account for every MCP client. This example publishes it only on the host loopback interface."},"212":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-131","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#docker","content":"The default HTTP address is 127.0.0.1:8082, which inside a container is unreachable from the outside, so IGGY_MCP_HTTP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8082 is required for the published port to work."},"213":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-132","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#systemd-integration","content":"Build with the systemd cargo feature to enable systemd readiness and watchdog notifications:"},"214":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-133","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#systemd-integration","content":"Readiness is sent after the HTTP listener starts or the stdio MCP session initializes. When systemd enables the watchdog, the server sends keep-alive notifications at half the configured watchdog interval. SIGINT, SIGTERM, or stdio client disconnect stops the server and sends a stopping notification."},"215":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"page","content":"Server-to-server","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Binary Protocol"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster"},"216":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-0","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","content":"The replica-to-replica plane: its dedicated TCP port, command discriminants, and traffic that never reaches a client."},"217":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-1","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#transport","content":"Transport"},"218":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-2","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#command-discriminants","content":"Command discriminants"},"219":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-3","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#frame-integrity","content":"Frame integrity"},"220":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-4","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"Handshake"},"221":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-5","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#replication","content":"Replication"},"222":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-6","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#prepare-6","content":"Prepare (6)"},"223":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-7","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#prepareok-7","content":"PrepareOk (7)"},"224":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-8","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#commit-9","content":"Commit (9)"},"225":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-9","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#view-changes","content":"View changes"},"226":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-10","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#journal-repair","content":"Journal repair"},"227":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-11","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"State transfer"},"228":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-12","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#session-forwarding","content":"Session forwarding"},"229":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-13","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#consensus-planes","content":"Consensus planes"},"230":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-14","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","content":"Replicas talk to each other with the same 256-byte framing the client protocol uses, on a dedicated TCP port, with their own set of command discriminants. Client listeners reject replica control commands. The replica listener requires a handshake; peer authentication is optional and must be configured separately."},"231":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-15","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","content":"This page documents the replica plane used with binary protocol 0.11.0. These internal frames do not negotiate a replica protocol version. Use compatible server builds and follow the cluster upgrade requirements."},"232":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-16","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#transport","content":"Every node in [[cluster.nodes]] exposes a tcp_replica port next to the client ports: ports = { tcp = 8090, quic = 8080, http = 3000, websocket = 8092, tcp_replica = 9090 }. The node's ip is the roster address for the replica plane (clients fall back to it when advertised_address is unset)."},"233":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-17","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#transport","content":"The replica plane is TCP only, by design: the prepare hash chain, cross-shard fd delegation, and view-change timing all assume an ordered byte stream."},"234":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-18","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#transport","content":"Directional dialing: a replica dials only peers with a strictly greater replica_id and accepts inbound connections only from strictly lower ids. This gives each connected pair one dialing direction, avoiding simultaneous dial races."},"235":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-19","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#transport","content":"Frames are the standard [256-byte header][optional body] with size at offset 48. The maximum frame is 64 MiB by default (message_bus.max_message_size). Headers are #[repr(C)], decoded zero-copy, and require 16-byt