ntent":"Update user. Code: 35."},"556":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-85","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Update permissions. Code: 36."},"557":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-86","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"As in CreateUser, the length field is conditional: with has_permissions = 0 the payload ends at the flag."},"558":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-87","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Change password. Code: 37."},"559":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-88","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Login and logout are part of the connection lifecycle:"},"560":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-89","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Login register. Code: 40. Operation::Register. The body is ClientVersionInfo + credentials, described in Login-register."},"561":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-90","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Login register with PAT. Code: 45. Operation::Register. The body is ClientVersionInfo + token."},"562":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-91","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Logout user. Code: 39. Operation::Logout. Empty payload."},"563":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-92","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Codes 38 and 44 are the pre-register login shapes. The server rejects both with a MalformedLogin eviction."},"564":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-93","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"Get personal access tokens. Code: 41. Empty payload."},"565":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-94","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"Create personal access token. Code: 42."},"566":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-95","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"expiry is a duration in microseconds. Two sentinel values exist: 0 (server default, which currently means no expiry) and u64::MAX (never expires)."},"567":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-96","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"Delete personal access token. Code: 43."},"568":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","type":"page","content":"Connection lifecycle","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Binary Protocol"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle"},"569":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-0","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","content":"How the protocol version is packed and checked, and the login-register handshake that authenticates a connection."},"570":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-1","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#protocol-version","content":"Protocol version"},"571":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-2","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"Login-register"},"572":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-3","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#protocol-version","content":"The protocol version is a semver packed into one u32, 10 bits per component (each must be below 1024):"},"573":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-4","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#protocol-version","content":"Integer order equals semver order. The value tracks the iggy_binary_protocol crate release. Under 0.x the compatibility gate is minor-scoped: the server accepts a client whose packed version is at least the server's minimum and whose major.minor is at most the server's. Patch releases never change the wire, so the upper bound ignores patch. The implemented minimum is the current version with its patch component zeroed. Compatibility across minor versions after 1.0.0 requires a future change to that calculation."},"574":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-5","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"The only way to authenticate is the register handshake: command code 40 (LOGIN_REGISTER, username and password) or 45 (LOGIN_REGISTER_WITH_PAT, personal access token). Both ride operation = 1 (Register) with session = 0, request = 0, and a non-zero client id. Mint a fresh id for a new logical client. A client resuming an existing identity must retain its request counter and use the new session epoch returned by registration."},"575":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-6","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"Both request bodies begin with the ClientVersionInfo prefix, so the server can gate on the version before touching credentials:"},"576":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-7","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"protocol_version is the packed version of the protocol the client was built against. sdk_name identifies the SDK (for example rust-sdk, go-sdk). sdk_version is the SDK's own build version."},"577":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-8","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"After the prefix:"},"578":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-9","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"LOGIN_REGISTER (code 40):"},"579":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-10","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"LOGIN_REGISTER_WITH_PAT (code 45):"},"580":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-11","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"An incompatible protocol version is answered with a 256-byte Eviction frame, reason 14 (IncompatibleProtocol), carrying the accepted window at bytes 144 (max) and 148 (min) as packed u32 versions. A body without a decodable ClientVersionInfo prefix gets reason 15 (MalformedLogin) with a zero window. Bad credentials get reasons 9-11. See EvictionHeader for the frame layout."},"581":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-12","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"A successful login is a normal Reply with status = 0. Its body includes an empty result section before the login payload:"},"582":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-13","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"The client stores session and echoes it in every subsequent request header. Logout is command code 39 (LOGOUT_USER), operation = 3 (Logout), empty body."},"583":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-14","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"The legacy login commands 38 (LOGIN_USER) and 44 (LOGIN_WITH_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN) are refused: the server answers them with an Eviction frame, reason 15 (MalformedLogin). Only PING is accepted before login. Every other command on an unauthenticated connection is denied."},"584":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"page","content":"Shared encodings","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Binary Protocol"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings"},"585":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-0","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","content":"The primitives command payloads are built from: names, identifiers, consumers and the other shared encodings."},"586":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-1","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#name","content":"Name"},"587":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-2","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#iden