ng#operation-discriminants","content":"Metadata operations replicate through the metadata consensus group. The server deduplicates them by (client, request) within its retained client history. Recent replies are cached and replayed; an older request can remain recognized as already applied after its reply has left the cache. This is not an unlimited exactly-once guarantee."},"902":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-218","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#operation-discriminants","content":"Partition operations normally replicate through their partition's consensus group. Each partition retains client request watermarks and a 128-id committed window to suppress duplicates. There is no reply cache: a recognized duplicate gets an empty success, including no offset confirmations for a retried send. Capacity eviction or a new client identity loses this coverage. Single-replica consumer-offset writes with ack = 0 bypass consensus and this deduplication."},"903":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-219","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#operation-discriminants","content":"Non-replicated operations bypass consensus and deduplication. A poll can still submit a separate consumer-offset write when auto-commit is enabled."},"904":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-220","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"request is a per-client counter the server's client table tracks for metadata-plane operations:"},"905":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-221","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"New metadata operations must use a request above the retained watermark; a retry reuses its original number and arguments."},"906":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-222","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"The Rust SDK advances one shared counter for metadata operations, partition operations, logout, and DeleteSegments. Non-replicated operations use the current value without advancing it."},"907":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-223","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"Register uses request = 0; other operations except NonReplicated require a non-zero request number."},"908":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-224","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"The counter is a watermark, not a contiguous sequence: any value above the last accepted one is admissible."},"909":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-225","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"session is the fence epoch handed back by the login reply, derived from the committed Register log position. Echo it after login. Registering the same client again mints a higher epoch; the metadata client table rejects an older epoch. Partition dispatch uses the identity bound to the authenticated connection."},"910":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-226","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"The partition window is measured in the shared client request-id space. An unseen request arriving 128 or more ids below that partition's watermark is treated as already committed and receives success without executing. Avoid allowing an unresolved write to fall outside that window; the protocol does not provide an unlimited reordered-retry guarantee."},"911":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-227","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Server to client. 256 bytes, followed by size - 256 bytes of body."},"912":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-228","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Offset"},"913":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-229","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Size"},"914":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-230","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Field"},"915":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-231","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Type"},"916":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-232","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Description"},"917":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-233","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"0"},"918":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-234","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"16"},"919":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-235","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"checksum"},"920":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-236","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"u128"},"921":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-237","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Zer