Polled messages can be replayed later, for example into another topic:"},"1341":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-82","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#message-flush","content":"message flush and -f, --fsync remain accepted by the CLI, but the server returns FeatureUnavailable on binary transports and has no HTTP flush route. This command does not flush data. Select --durability persisted when creating the topic to require stable-storage message acknowledgments."},"1342":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-83","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#user","content":"Manage users and their permissions. Subcommands: create, delete, get, list, name, status, password, permissions."},"1343":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-84","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#permissions","content":"user create and user permissions accept the same permission flags:"},"1344":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-85","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#permissions","content":"Flag"},"1345":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-86","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#permissions","content":"Description"},"1346":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-87","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#permissions","content":"-g, --global-permissions <PERMS>"},"1347":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-88","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#permissions","content":"Comma-separated global permissions"},"1348":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-89","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#permissions","content":"-s, --stream-permissions <SPEC>"},"1349":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-90","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#permissions","content":"Per-stream (and per-topic) permissions, repeatable"},"1350":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-91","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#permissions","content":"-u, --user-status <active|inactive>"},"1351":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-92","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#permissions","content":"Initial status (create only, default: active)"},"1352":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-93","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#permissions","content":"Global permission tokens (long / short): manage_servers/m_srv, read_servers/r_srv, manage_users/m_usr, read_users/r_usr, manage_streams/m_str, read_streams/r_str, manage_topics/m_top, read_topics/r_top, poll_messages/p_msg, send_messages/s_msg."},"1353":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-94","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#permissions","content":"The stream permission format is STREAM_ID[:STREAM_PERMISSIONS][#TOPIC_ID[:TOPIC_PERMISSIONS]]:"},"1354":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-95","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#pat","content":"Manage personal access tokens for the authenticated user. Subcommands: create, delete, list."},"1355":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-96","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#pat","content":"--store-token and an expiry are mutually exclusive: stored tokens never expire."},"1356":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-97","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#client","content":"Inspect the clients currently connected to the server. Clients are connections, not users."},"1357":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-98","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#cluster","content":"Cluster operations. The only subcommand is metadata (alias m)."},"1358":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-99","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#consumer-group","content":"Manage consumer groups of a topic. Subcommands: create, delete, get, list."},"1359":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-100","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#consumer-offset","content":"Read or set the stored offset of a consumer for one partition. Subcommands: get, set. Both accept -k, --kind <consumer|consumer-group> (default: consumer)."},"1360":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-101","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#context","content":"Manage named connection profiles. Subcommands: list, use, create, delete, show. See connection contexts for details."},"1361":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-102","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#options","content":"Print the server's option catalog for one scope: topic, stream or user, with key kinds, defaults, and bounds. Only the topic catalog has entries, and only topic create exposes --set KEY=VALUE in the CLI."},"1362":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-103","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#ping","content":"Check that the server is up and measure the response time. -c, --count <N> sends N pings (default: 1)."},"1363":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-104","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#me","content":"Show info about the current connection: client ID, user ID, client address as seen by the server, protocol. Supported on TCP, QUIC and WebSocket; HTTP returns FeatureUnavailable."},"1364":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-105","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#stats","content":"Show server statistics: streams, topics, partitions, messages, plus host OS details. -o, --output <table|list|json|toml> selects the format (default: table)."},"1365":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-106","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#snapshot","content":"Collect server troubleshooting data into an archive, useful for support bundles."},"1366":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-107","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#snapshot","content":"Flag"},"1367":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-108","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#snapshot","content":"Description"},"1368":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-109","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#snapshot","content":"-c, --compression <TYPE>"},"1369":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-110","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#snapshot","content":"stored, deflated, bzip2, zstd, lzma, xz"},"1370":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-111","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#snapshot","content":"-s, --snapshot-types <TYPES>"},"1371":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-112","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#snapshot","content":"Space-separated subset of filesystem_overview, process_list, resource_usage, test, server_logs, server_config, all"},"1372":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-113","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#snapshot","content":"-o, --out-dir <DIR>"},"1373":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-114","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#snapshot","content":"Existing output directory for the snapshot file"},"1374":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-115","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#login-logout-session","content":"Manage login sessions backed by the platform keyring. iggy login [EXPIRY] stores a session token (default expiry: 15 minutes, none disables expiry), iggy logout removes it, and iggy session status reports whether a token exists locally. See login sessions for the full flow."},"1375":{"id":"/docs/cli/start","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"page","content":"Iggy CLI","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","CLI"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start"},"1376":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-0","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start","content":"Install the Iggy CLI, connect to a server, authenticate, and manage connection contexts."},"1377":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-1","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#installation","content":"Installation"},"1378":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-2","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#cargo","content":"Cargo"},"1379":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-3","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#docker","content":"Docker"},"1380":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-4","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"Connecting to a server"},"1381":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-5","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"TLS and encryption"},"1382":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-6","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"Authentication"},"1383":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-7","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#login-sessions","content":"Login sessions"},"1384":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-8","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#personal-access-tokens","content":"Personal access tokens"},"1385":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-9","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#connection-contexts","content":"Connection contexts"},"1386":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-10","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#shell-completions","content":"Shell completions"},"1387":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-11","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start","content":"The Iggy CLI is a command-line interface for managing an Iggy server: streams, topics, partitions, messages, users, tokens and more. It lives in the core repository under the core/cli directory. The crate is named iggy-cli, the installed binary iggy."},"1388":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-12","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start","content":"This page covers installing the CLI, connecting to a server, authenticating, and managing connection contexts. The full command tree is documented in the command reference."},"1389":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-13","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#cargo","content":"These docs target server 0.9.0 and CLI 0.14.0, which was released with it. The CLI has its own version number, so use the version that matches your server. Install it from crates.io with:"},"1390":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-14","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#cargo","content":"This builds and installs the iggy binary. With cargo-binstall, you can download a prebuilt binary instead. It builds from source if there isn't one for your platform:"},"1391":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-15","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#cargo","content":"To build it from source instead, use the same checkout as your server and run this from its root:"},"1392":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-16","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#docker","content":"The official apache/iggy image ships the CLI alongside the server, installed as /usr/local/bin/iggy. The image entrypoint is the server, so override it to run the CLI:"},"1393":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-17","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#docker","content":"--network host works on Linux. On macOS and Windows, point the CLI at the host instead: --tcp-server-address host.docker.internal:8090."},"1394":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-18","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#docker","content":"Once installed, run iggy without arguments to print an overview of the available commands, or see the command reference."},"1395":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-19","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"The CLI talks to the server over one of four transports, selected with --transport. Accepted values are tcp, quic, http and ws (not websocket). The default is tcp."},"1396":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-20","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"Transport"},"1397":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-21","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"Address flag"},"1398":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-22","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"Default"},"1399":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-23","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"tcp"},"1400":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-24","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"--tcp-server-address"},"1401":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-25","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"127.0.0.1:8090"},"1402":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-26","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"quic"},"1403":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-27","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"--quic-server-address"},"1404":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-28","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"127.0.0.1:8080"},"1405":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-29","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"http"},"1406":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-30","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"--http-api-url"},"1407":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-31","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"http://localhost:3000"},"1408":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-32","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"ws"},"1409":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-33","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"--websocket-server-address"},"1410":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-34","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"127.0.0.1:8092"},"1411":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-35","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"Flag"},"1412":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-36","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"Description"},"1413":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-37","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"--tcp-tls-enabled"},"1414":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-38","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"Enable TLS for the TCP transport"},"1415":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-39","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"--tcp-tls-domain"},"1416":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-40","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"TLS domain for the TCP transport (default: localhost)"},"1417":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-41","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"--quic-validate-certificate"},"1418":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-42","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"Enable certificate validation for QUIC"},"1419":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-43","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"--encryption-key"},"1420":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-44","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"Base64-encoded AES-256-GCM key for client-side message payload encryption"},"1421":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-45","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"Each transport also exposes reconnection and tuning flags (retry counts, intervals, QUIC window sizes and MTU). Run iggy --help for the complete list with defaults."},"1422":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-46","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"Broker commands require credentials, except for ping. For commands other than login, the CLI resolves credentials in this order, first match wins:"},"1423":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-47","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"A cached login session token (created by iggy login)."},"1424":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-48","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"-n, --token-name <name>: a personal access token stored in the platform keyring under that name."},"1425":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-49","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"-t, --token <token>: a personal access token passed directly."},"1426":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-50","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"-u, --username <user> with -p, --password <password>. When -p is omitted, the CLI prompts for the password interactively (or reads one line from stdin when piped)."},"1427":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-51","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"The IGGY_USERNAME and IGGY_PASSWORD environment variables (both must be set)."},"1428":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-52","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"iggy login tries the supplied credentials before a cached session, so it can replace an expired login. Other commands fail if their cached token is rejected; they do not retry with the next credential source in the same invocation."},"1429":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-53","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"-u, -t and -n are mutually exclusive. Avoid passing the password inline with -p: it lands in your shell history. Prefer iggy login, the interactive prompt, or the environment variables."},"1430":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-54","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#login-sessions","content":"iggy login authenticates once and stores a session token in the platform's secure credential store: Secret Service on Linux and the BSDs, Keychain on macOS, Credential Manager on Windows. Subsequent commands use the cached token automatically. This requires the default login-session feature and an available credential-store backend; Linux and BSD need a running Secret Service provider on the D-Bus session."},"1431":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-55","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#login-sessions","content":"iggy session status only checks whether a token exists in the local keyring. An expired token still reports as active. On TCP, QUIC and WebSocket, run iggy me to verify the session against the server. HTTP does not support me; use an authenticated command such as iggy stream list with the required permissions."},"1432":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-56","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#personal-access-tokens","content":"Create a token once, then authenticate with it instead of a username and password:"},"1433":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-57","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#personal-access-tokens","content":"--store-token is mutually exclusive with an expiry: stored tokens never expire. They're also namespaced per server address, so a token stored for one server isn't visible when connecting to another. Alternatively, create a token with an expiry and pass its value with -t:"},"1434":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-58","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connection-contexts","content":"Contexts are named connection profiles (transport, addresses, TLS settings, credentials) for working with multiple environments. Subcommands: list, use, create, delete, show."},"1435":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-59","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connection-contexts","content":"Creating and activating a context are two separate steps:"},"1436":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-60","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connection-contexts","content":"There's no --context flag: the active context is persistent state, switched with iggy context use <name>. Flags passed on the command line override the corresponding values from the active context. Credential fields merge independently: supplying --username does not clear a context token, which still has higher authentication priority."},"1437":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-61","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connection-contexts","content":"Contexts are stored in contexts.toml, and the active context name in .active_context, both under the Iggy home directory: ~/.iggy by default, overridable with the IGGY_HOME environment variable. The default context always exists and cannot be deleted or recreated through context commands. Deleting the currently active context switches back to default. Context names may contain letters, digits, hyphens and underscores. Passwords and raw tokens supplied to context create are stored as plaintext in contexts.toml (owner-only permissions on Unix); use a stored token name when the credential should stay in the keyring."},"1438":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-62","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#shell-completions","content":"Generate completions for bash, zsh, fish, elvish or powershell with --generate:"},"1439":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-63","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#shell-completions","content":"For zsh, add ~/.zfunc to fpath before running compinit in your shell configuration."},"1440":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","type":"page","content":"Client Failover","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Clustering"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover"},"1441":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-0","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","content":"Local reads, leader redirection, and retryable errors from the client side"},"1442":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-1","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#where-requests-are-served","content":"Where requests are served"},"1443":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-2","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#leader-redirection","content":"Leader redirection"},"1444":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-3","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#retryable-errors","content":"Retryable errors"},"1445":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-4","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#sdk-support","content":"SDK support"},"1446":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-5","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","content":"A clustered deployment changes what a client can expect from any single node: reads are served locally where possible, writes must reach the right primary, and the SDKs follow the primary as it moves."},"1447":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-6","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#where-requests-are-served","content":"Reads use the local replicated state by default on whichever node owns the namespace locally, followers included. A client connected to a follower can poll messages and read metadata without touching the primary."},"1448":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-7","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#where-requests-are-served","content":"Writes go through consensus and are admitted on the plane's primary only: metadata operations on the metadata primary, message and offset writes on the primary of that partition's consensus group. An acknowledged write returns after the required VSR commit, or returns a retryable error when the replica is changing view or catching up. HTTP ack=none returns after dispatch instead."},"1449":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-8","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#where-requests-are-served","content":"One caveat on follower reads: server-managed offset auto-commit replicates the polled offset through the partition consensus, which only the partition primary may do. A poll served by a follower does not advance the durable consumer offset. Auto-commit is best-effort and the poll response does not await offset persistence. On a primary, the offset submission precedes delivery of the poll reply, so it does not guarantee that the application received or processed the batch. Store offsets explicitly after processing when that ordering is required."},"1450":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-9","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#leader-redirection","content":"Leader-aware SDKs discover the metadata primary. Rust binary clients follow this sequence:"},"1451":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-10","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#leader-redirection","content":"The client connects and authenticates to its configured node, then fetches cluster metadata, which lists every node with its name, client endpoints, role, and status."},"1452":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-11","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#leader-redirection","content":"When the connected node isn't the leader, the client reconnects to the leader's advertised endpoint for the transport in use."},"1453":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-12","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#leader-redirection","content":"When the cluster is transiently leaderless, the client polls metadata every 250ms for up to 5s. If no healthy leader appears within that window, it continues on the contacted node and lets subsequent requests report their outcome."},"1454":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-13","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#leader-redirection","content":"Redirects are capped (three in the Rust SDK) so a flapping roster can't bounce the client forever."},"1455":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-14","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#leader-redirection","content":"The address a client is redirected to is the node's advertised_address (or the matching advertised_addresses selector), falling back to the roster ip. Getting those right matters: the client redials exactly what the metadata advertises. See Configuration for the selector rules."},"1456":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-15","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#leader-redirection","content":"Metadata and partition primaries can differ. After a TransientNotAccepted response, Rust binary clients check the metadata leader and can then try the remaining advertised endpoints in a bounded roster walk. This fallback is separate from the three-redirect limit."},"1457":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-16","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#leader-redirection","content":"The Rust implementation lives in core/sdk/src/leader_aware.rs."},"1458":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-17","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#retryable-errors","content":"During a view change or while a replica catches up, writes can return transient errors. Rust binary clients retry within their request budgets. TransientNotAccepted proves that the request was never admitted and permits retry on another node. TransientNotCommitted has an uncertain outcome and is replayed with the same session and request identity, then returned if the budget expires. Disconnects and exhausted routing attempts can still reach the caller. Definitive validation and permission errors are not replayed."},"1459":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-18","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#sdk-support","content":"Every SDK speaks the VSR wire protocol and can connect to any node of a cluster. Client-side leader redirection is exercised by the shared leader_redirection BDD suite, which runs against a real two-node cluster:"},"1460":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-19","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#sdk-support","content":"SDK"},"1461":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-20","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#sdk-support","content":"VSR wire protocol"},"1462":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-21","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#sdk-support","content":"Leader redirection suite"},"1463":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-22","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#sdk-support","content":"Rust"},"1464":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-23","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#sdk-support","content":"yes"},"1465":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-24","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#sdk-support","content":"yes"},"1466":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-25","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#sdk-support","content":"Go"},"1467":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-26","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#sdk-support","content":"yes"},"1468":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-27","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[