crumbs":["Documentation","Tutorials"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service"},"2868":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","content":"Build a small order-processing service in Python: key messages by customer, share a topic between two service instances with a consumer group, and see them take over from each other and resume."},"2869":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#before-we-start","content":"Before we start"},"2870":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#start-the-server","content":"Start the server"},"2871":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"The producer"},"2872":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-4","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-service","content":"The service"},"2873":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-5","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"Run it"},"2874":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-6","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#running-it-again","content":"Running it again"},"2875":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-7","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","content":"You will build a small order-processing service. A producer writes order events to a topic, keyed by customer. Two copies of the service share the topic through a consumer group. You stop one and watch the other take over, then start it again and see it resume where it left off."},"2876":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-8","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#before-we-start","content":"You need Docker, Python 3.10 to 3.13, and the Python SDK in a virtual environment. On Linux the server needs kernel 5.19 or newer, but macOS has no kernel requirement (see System requirements)."},"2877":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-9","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#before-we-start","content":"Consumer groups need a binary transport, so this tutorial uses TCP. HTTP cannot join a group (see Consumer groups and the FAQ)."},"2878":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-10","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#start-the-server","content":"The flags are explained on Docker & Helm. IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all turns off NUMA binding, which Docker Desktop does not support."},"2879":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-11","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"Save as orders_producer.py."},"2880":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-12","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"Partitioning.messages_key hashes the key to pick the partition, so orders for one customer share a partition and stay in order. The connection string comes from the environment, so the same script works against a server on another host (see Connection strings)."},"2881":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-13","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"Run it:"},"2882":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-14","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"This is the output from one run. Yours will differ because customers are picked at random."},"2883":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-15","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-service","content":"Save as order_service.py."},"2884":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-16","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-service","content":"consume_messages runs until the shutdown event is set. Ctrl-C, or a SIGTERM from a supervisor, sets it, and the current message finishes before the call returns. The instance name is only used in the log."},"2885":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-17","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-service","content":"AutoCommit.After stores the offset once the handler returns, even if it raised an exception. Handle errors inside handle, or a failed message is skipped rather than retried."},"2886":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-18","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"One instance. Start the service in a second terminal:"},"2887":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-19","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"It is the only member, so it gets all three partitions and drains what the producer wrote:"},"2888":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-20","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"Scale out. In a third terminal start a second instance, then run the producer again:"},"2889":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-21","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"The server rebalances the group. In this run svc-b took partition 2 and svc-a kept 0 and 1, and no order was printed by both."},"2890":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-22","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"Scale in. Press Ctrl-C in the svc-a terminal. It prints [svc-a] shutting down and exits. Run the producer again. svc-b now owns every partition and receives all 15 orders (output trimmed):"},"2891":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-23","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"Resume. Start svc-a again. It prints only its joined line, because the group's stored offsets mean nothing is replayed. Run the producer once more and svc-a gets partition 2 back:"},"2892":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-24","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"When a partition moves, the server stops the old owner polling it before handing it to the new one, and waits up to 30 seconds by default (see How does consumer group rebalancing work?)."},"2893":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-25","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#running-it-again","content":"The producer creates the stream and topic only if they are missing, so it can be run any number of times. A restarted service resumes from the group's stored offset. To start from nothing, stop the container, and --rm removes it along with its data."},"2894":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-26","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#running-it-again","content":"The service is the same consumer shape as the high-level consumer example in the repository, with a JSON handler and signal handling added."},"2895":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"page","content":"A Grafana dashboard for the broker","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Tutorials"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard"},"2896":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","content":"Run an Iggy server with Prometheus and Grafana in Docker Compose, and watch the broker on a dashboard."},"2897":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#before-we-start","content":"Before we start"},"2898":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#the-stack","content":"The stack"},"2899":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#start-it","content":"Start it"},"2900":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-4","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#create-a-token-for-prometheus","content":"Create a token for Prometheus"},"2901":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-5","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafan