es#plugin-config-options","content":"string"},"5453":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-112","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#plugin-config-options","content":"\"1s\""},"5454":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-113","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#plugin-config-options","content":"Linear delay: this duration times the failed attempt number; invalid values fall back to 1s"},"5455":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-114","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#polling-and-delivery","content":"The default query selects rows with tracking_column > last_offset, orders that column ascending, and applies batch_size separately to each table. Use a non-null, unique tracking value that increases in the database's sort order. A timestamp with ties can skip rows at a batch boundary, and inserts or updates at or below the watermark are not revisited. Polling does not capture deletions."},"5456":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-115","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#polling-and-delivery","content":"A custom_query replaces the entire default query, including its ordering, limit and processed-row filter. Its placeholders are $table, $offset, $limit, $now (RFC3339 UTC) and $now_unix (integer seconds). Substitution is textual, with no automatic quoting or escaping; $offset is empty if neither saved state nor initial_offset supplies it. Keep queries trusted and explicitly preserve ascending tracking-column order. The offset comes from the last returned row with a usable tracking value."},"5457":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-116","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#polling-and-delivery","content":"Polling stages its cursor and processed-row count until the runtime forwards the batch, saves its checkpoint and sends Ack. Nack discards that candidate so the next poll can read the rows again. A failure after forwarding can therefore duplicate messages. IDs are random UUIDs for each poll, so replayed rows do not retain a stable message ID."},"5458":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-117","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#polling-and-delivery","content":"delete_after_read and processed_column stage cleanup keys during polling and modify PostgreSQL only after Ack. A Nack or failed poll leaves the rows available for replay. Cleanup is separate from delivery and checkpointing: a cleanup error stops the source, and a crash after checkpointing but before cleanup can leave already-delivered rows unmodified in PostgreSQL. Cleanup across tables is not atomic."},"5459":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-118","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#polling-and-delivery","content":"The file state backend stores source_postgres-source.state under the runtime's state path (default local_state). Its MessagePack state contains per-table offsets, a last-poll time and a processed-row count. The HTTP state backend is also available. Missing or undecodable plugin state starts fresh; file access errors prevent startup. Stop the runtime before resetting its checkpoint. initial_offset is used only where saved offsets are absent."},"5460":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-119","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#polling-and-delivery","content":"The plugin's retries cover transient polling SELECT failures, including connection I/O, pool timeouts, serialization failures and deadlocks. Connection setup, CDC fetches and delete/mark statements do not use that retry loop. A poll error is logged and polling continues without incrementing the runtime's forwarding-error counter. Repeated delivery or checkpoint failures instead receive Nack; the SDK stops after five consecutive Nacks."},"5461":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-120","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#output-format","content":"By default (no payload_column), each row is wrapped in a JSON envelope:"},"5462":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-121","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#output-format","content":"The stream config should use schema = \"json\". Flat-schema sinks such as Iceberg need the unwrap_envelope transform to extract the data field. See the transforms documentation."},"5463":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-122","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#payload-column-extraction","content":"In polling mode, an existing payload_column bypasses the envelope. Use bytea or raw for a BYTEA column with schema = \"raw\", text for a text column with schema = \"text\", and json_direct, jsonb or jsonb_direct for JSON/JSONB with schema = \"json\". A null BYTEA/text payload becomes empty bytes, which the Iggy message builder rejects; the entire batch is Nacked. Use non-null, non-empty BYTEA/text payloads. A null JSON/JSONB payload becomes JSON null and can be delivered."},"5464":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-123","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#payload-column-extraction","content":"The default payload_format = \"json\" reads a selected column as BYTEA but reports JSON to the runtime, so those bytes must contain valid JSON. Without a selected column, the connector always emits a JSON envelope. If the selected column is absent, it falls back to the envelope while retaining the selected format's schema; use a column present in every configured table. Type mismatches reject the poll. See the upstream README for sink round-trip recipes."},"5465":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-124","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#payload-column-extraction","content":"With no selected column, include_metadata = false currently retains the envelope and adds an extra data level. Whole-row BYTEA fields are base64 strings. Keep database timestamps in the payload when needed; the runtime does not copy the plugin's timestamp fields into broker message metadata."},"5466":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-125","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#cdc-mode","content":"With mode = \"cdc\" the connector reads changes from a logical replication slot instead of polling tables:"},"5467":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-126","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#cdc-mode","content":"The builtin backend creates (or reuses) a logical replication slot using the test_decoding output plugin. The default slot name is iggy_slot. No publication is created or required, since test_decoding ignores publications entirely."},"5468":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-127","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#cdc-mode","content":"PostgreSQL must run with wal_level = logical and have test_decoding installed and allowed. The login needs access to the logical-decoding SQL functions. This backend uses ordinary SQL connections, not a replication-protocol connection; any proxy must support its SQL and slot operations."},"5469":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-128","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#cdc-mode","content":"capture_operations accepts uppercase INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE; an empty array emits none. Filtering happens after reading the slot. Qualified table names match the schema and table; unqualified names match that table name in any schema. An empty tables array captures all tables."},"5470":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-129","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#cdc-mode","content":"The pg_replicate backend (based on Supabase's ETL framework) is not implemented: selecting cdc_backend = \"pg_replicate\" fails at startup unless the cdc_pg_replicate build feature is enabled, and the backend returns an error even when it is."},"5471":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-130","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#cdc-mode","content":"Each connector needs its own slot. Reusing one slot across connectors divides its changes between them. pg_logical_slot_get_changes consumes slot progress before delivery, including changes excluded by the filters. A later failure cannot replay those changes from the slot, and the plugin checkpoint does not contain a recovery LSN."},"5472":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-131","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#cdc-mode","content":"CDC always builds a JSON envelope with the operation and parsed columns. Do not combine it with polling payload extraction options. Deletes contain replica-identity columns; updates may also supply old_data from an old-key tuple. Quoted test_decoding values, including JSONB and arrays, remain strings. Unchanged TOAST values are represented as null, which does not establish that the database value is null. Envelope timestamps are generated while polling, not PostgreSQL transaction commit times."},"5473":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres-132","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres#cdc-mode","content":"When decommissioning a CDC connector, stop it and drop its replication slot to stop WAL retention. With PostgreSQL's default max_slot_wal_keep_size = -1, an unused slot can retain unbounded WAL. See the upstream README for details."},"5474":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","type":"page","content":"Random Source","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Connectors","Sources"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random"},"5475":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-0","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","content":"Generate random messages into Iggy streams for testing, benchmarking and verifying a connector pipeline."},"5476":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-1","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#configuration","content":"Configuration"},"5477":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-2","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"Plugin config options"},"5478":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-3","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","content":"The random source connector generates random messages and sends them to Iggy streams. This is useful for testing, benchmarking, and verifying that your connector pipeline is working correctly without needing an external data source."},"5479":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-4","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#configuration","content":"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the source guide. Save the configuration below in that runtime's connector directory. From the matching 0.9.0/edge repository root, build the plugin:"},"5480":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-5","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"Option"},"5481":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-6","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"Type"},"5482":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-7","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"Default"},"5483":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-8","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"Description"},"5484":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-9","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"interval"},"5485":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-10","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"string"},"5486":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-11","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"\"1s\""},"5487":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-12","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"Delay before each message generation cycle; invalid duration strings fall back to \"1s\""},"5488":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-13","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"max_count"},"5489":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-14","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"usize"},"5490":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-15","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"unlimited"},"5491":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-16","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"Stop producing after this many messages in total; omit for unlimited"},"5492":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-17","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"messages_range"},"5493":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-18","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"[u32, u32]"},"5494":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-19","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"[10, 50]"},"5495":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-20","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"Min (inclusive) / max (exclusive) messages per batch, capped by the remaining max_count; the lower bound must be smaller"},"5496":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-21","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"payload_size"},"5497":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-22","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"u32"},"5498":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-23","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"100"},"5499":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-24","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"Number of random characters in the text field of each message"},"5500":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-25","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"Setting max_count = 0 stops production immediately (the connector produces zero messages). To generate messages without a limit, leave the key out."},"5501":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-26","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"The connector stages its count for each batch and commits it only after the runtime acknowledges delivery and saves the checkpoint. Restoring that checkpoint enforces max_count across restarts; missing or undecodable state starts the count at zero. After reaching the limit, polling continues but returns no messages or new checkpoint."},"5502":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-27","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"A non-increasing messages_range returns a configuration error when message generation is attempted. The SDK logs poll errors and continues polling. Each generated JSON record contains a random UUID id, fixed title = \"Hello\" and name = \"World\", and random alphanumeric text. payload_size controls only that text, not the total serialized record size."},"5503":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random-28","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/random#plugin-config-options","content":"Create my-stream and my-topic before starting the runtime, or change the sample to use an existing destination. Configure one destination per source instance."},"5504":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","type":"page","content":"Source","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Connectors","Sources"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source"},"5505":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-0","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","content":"What source connectors do, and the Source trait every one of them implements."},"5506":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-1","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#overview","content":"Overview"},"5507":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-2","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Configuration"},"5508":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-3","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#environment-variable-overrides","content":"Environment Variable Overrides"},"5509":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-4","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"Sample implementation"},"5510":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-5","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#overview","content":"Source connectors are responsible for ingesting data from external sources into Apache Iggy. They provide a way to integrate Apache Iggy with various data sources, such as databases, message queues, or file systems."},"5511":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-6","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#overview","content":"The source is represented by the single Source trait, which defines the basic interface for all source connectors. It provides methods for initializing the source, reading data from it, and closing the source."},"5512":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-7","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"With the local configuration provider, each source connector has a TOML file in the directory specified by the main runtime config. The runtime also supports an HTTP configuration provider."},"5513":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-8","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Field"},"5514":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-9","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Meaning"},"5515":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-10","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"key"},"5516":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-11","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Connector key (String), separate from the numeric plugin instance ID."},"5517":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-12","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"enabled"},"5518":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-13","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Whether to start the connector (bool)."},"5519":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-14","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"version"},"5520":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-15","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Configuration version (u64)."},"5521":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-16","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"name"},"5522":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-17","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Display name (String)."},"5523":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-18","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"path"},"5524":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-19","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Shared-library path (String)."},"5525":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-20","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"streams"},"5526":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-21","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Stream configuration entries."},"5527":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-22","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"transforms"},"5528":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-23","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Optional transform configuration."},"5529":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-24","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"plugin_config"},"5530":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-25","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Optional custom configuration object deserialized by the plugin."},"5531":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-26","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"plugin_config_format"},"5532":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-27","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Optional default format for the HTTP API's plugin-config response. The FFI always receives JSON."},"5533":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-28","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"verbose, benchmark"},"5534":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-29","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Optional logging flags (bool)."},"5535":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-30","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"verbose and benchmark are optional and default to false. verbose switches the connector's per-batch logging to info level, and benchmark emits per-batch timing events for performance measurement."},"5536":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-31","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Main runtime config (connectors.toml, in the repository root):"},"5537":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-32","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Use the matching 0.9.0/edge checkout and broker from the quick start. Its broker must use the credentials below. Create the connectors directory, then save these two configuration files there and in the repository root as labeled."},"5538":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-33","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Source connector config (connectors/random.toml):"},"5539":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-34","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#configuration","content":"Configure one [[streams]] destination per source instance. The runtime currently retains only the last producer when multiple entries are supplied."},"5540":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-35","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#environment-variable-overrides","content":"Configuration properties can be overridden using environment variables. Supported scalar fields and indexed stream entries use the pattern: IGGY_CONNECTORS_SOURCE_[KEY]_[PROPERTY]"},"5541":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-36","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#environment-variable-overrides","content":"For example, to disable the source with key random at the next runtime start:"},"5542":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-37","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#environment-variable-overrides","content":"Top-level fields of plugin_config can be overridden (or injected) the same way with the IGGY_CONNECTORS_SOURCE_[KEY]_PLUGIN_CONFIG_[FIELD] pattern. This lets you supply credentials without writing them into the TOML file:"},"5543":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-38","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"Let's implement the example source connector, which generates a random batch of messages at the configured interval and optionally stops after a total message limit."},"5544":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-39","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"Additionally, our source connector will have its own state, which can be used e.g. to track the overall progress or store some relevant information when producing the data from the actual external sources or tooling."},"5545":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-40","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"Keep in mind, that the produced messages will be sent further to the specified stream, however it's already the responsibility of the runtime to handle the delivery."},"5546":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-41","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"Also, when implementing the source connector, make sure to use the source_connector! macro to expose the FFI interface and allow the connector runtime to register the source with the runtime."},"5547":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-42","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"And finally, each source should have its own, custom configuration, which is passed along with the unique plugin ID and optional state via expected new() method."},"5548":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-43","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"The reference crate is core/connectors/sources/random_source. Use its Cargo.toml from the matching checkout, including [lib] crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"lib\"] and its workspace dependency features. The Rust blocks in this sample implementation section combine into src/lib.rs; the trait above is an API reference and is not part of that file."},"5549":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-44","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"Start with the imports, then define the internal state and the public source connector along with its configuration."},"5550":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-45","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"At this point, we can expose the required new() method, which will be used by the runtime to create a new instance of the source connector. The id is assigned by the runtime, and represents the unique identifier of the source connector. The state is an optional connector state (e.g. persisted by the file or HTTP state backend), which will be provided by the runtime, given that the connector has persisted its own state before the runtime was restarted. The ConnectorState::deserialize() helper decodes the MessagePack bytes back into our State struct, falling back to a fresh state when nothing was persisted or decoding fails."},"5551":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-46","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"We can invoke the expected macro to expose the FFI interface and allow the connector runtime to register the source within the runtime."},"5552":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-47","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"The reference manifest supplies these dependencies:"},"5553":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-48","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"async-trait"},"5554":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-49","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"dashmap"},"5555":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-50","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"iggy_connector_sdk"},"5556":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-51","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"serde"},"5557":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-52","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"tokio"},"5558":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-53","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"tracing"},"5559":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-54","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"The implementation also uses humantime, rand, simd-json, and uuid. The reference crate uses rmp-serde directly in its tests; the production state helpers come from the SDK."},"5560":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-55","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"Before we make use of the Source trait, let's define the internal payload of the message that will be produced (e.g. as if it was pulled from some external database or so)."},"5561":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-56","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"serialize_state() encodes the state to MessagePack via ConnectorState::serialize(). generate_messages() samples the half-open messages_range (lower bound included, upper bound excluded), capped by the remaining max_count. Each message carries a JSON-serialized Record with payload_size random alphanumeric characters of text. A non-increasing range returns Error::InvalidConfigValue from poll() without advancing state."},"5562":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-57","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"Now implement Source for RandomSource. Each poll() waits for interval, builds a batch without exceeding max_count, and stages a candidate checkpoint. The runtime sends the batch, saves its optional checkpoint, and calls on_batch_result(). Only Ack commits the in-memory count; Nack discards the candidate. After restart, new() receives the last persisted checkpoint. Once the limit is reached, polls return empty messages and no new state."},"5563":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-58","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"The SDK permits only one batch in flight. Returning an error from on_batch_result() stops polling; returning an error from poll() logs the error and continues the polling loop. A source that tracks a cursor or performs destructive upstream work must implement the callback instead of relying on its default no-op."},"5564":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-59","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"The runtime forwards the encoded payload, optional message ID and headers. Although ProducedMessage also accepts checksum, timestamp and origin_timestamp, the current forwarding path does not copy those three fields into the Iggy message."},"5565":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-60","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"It's also important to note, that the supported format(s) might vary depending on the connector implementation. For example, you might use JSON as the payload format, which can be then easily parsed and processed by downstream components such as data transforms, but at the same time, you could support the other formats and let the user decide which one to use."},"5566":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-61","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"The destination schema selects the StreamEncoder. A decode, transform or encoding error rejects the entire source batch, leaves its checkpoint uncommitted and produces Nack; the runtime logs and counts the error. An intentional filter result (Ok(None)) may drop selected messages without rejecting the batch."},"5567":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-62","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"Build the matching plugin, runtime and CLI from the repository root:"},"5568":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-63","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"On an empty broker, create the destination using the credentials from the quick start:"},"5569":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-64","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"After saving the configuration files above, start the runtime. This explicitly enables the source if you tried the optional disabling override:"},"5570":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-65","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"And that's all, enjoy using the source connector!"},"5571":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source-66","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/source","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/source#sample-implementation","content":"On a side note, if you'd like to process the messages consumed from the Iggy stream instead, you can implement your own Sink connector too :)"},"5572":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","type":"page","content":"C++ SDK (WIP)","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","C++"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro"},"5573":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","content":"The C++ SDK, a CXX bridge over the Rust SDK, and how to build it from source with Bazel."},"5574":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#building-from-source","content":"Building from source"},"5575":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"5576":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#connection-strings","content":"Connection strings"},"5577":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#helper-types","content":"Helper types"},"5578":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#current-status","content":"Current status"},"5579":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","content":"The Iggy C++ SDK wraps the Rust SDK through a CXX FFI bridge, so it can use all transport protocols (TCP, QUIC, HTTP, WebSocket) via connection strings. The source code is available at github.com/apache/iggy/tree/master/foreign/cpp."},"5580":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","content":"This SDK isn't published to any package registry. Consume it from the monorepo and build it from source with Bazel."},"5581":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#building-from-source","content":"The project builds with the Bazel version pinned in .bazelversion (BUILD.bazel / MODULE.bazel). Its Rust bridge uses the Cargo and Rust toolchain provisioned by rules_rust, with a separate target directory in the Bazel output tree. Use a compatible Java runtime for Bazel; the pinned Bazel 9.2.0 requires Java 21 or newer."},"5582":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#quick-start","content":"The snippet below mirrors the SDK's own end-to-end tests in foreign/cpp/tests/e2e. Start a local server on 127.0.0.1:8090 with the iggy/iggy credentials first. The stream name must not already exist."},"5583":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#quick-start","content":"Polling strategies are selected by kind string: offset, timestamp, first, last, or next. Partitioning kinds for sending are balanced, partition_id, and messages_key."},"5584":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#connection-strings","content":"The C++ SDK supports the same connection string format as the Rust SDK:"},"5585":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#connection-strings","content":"iggy::ffi::from_connection_string() accepts these strings. new_connection() instead takes an IggyClientConfig: {} selects TCP at 127.0.0.1:8090 without automatic login, and its server_address field overrides that address. Binary clients authenticate from their connection string during connect(); HTTP requires an explicit login_user() call. See connection strings for the supported options and credential syntax."},"5586":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#helper-types","content":"Beyond the generated lib.rs.h FFI surface, foreign/cpp/include/iggy.hpp ships typed C++ helpers: CompressionAlgorithm, MaxTopicSize, Expiry, PollingStrategy, IdKind, TopicOption, SnapshotCompression, and SystemSnapshotType, plus the IggyException error type. They construct and validate the kind strings and values the raw FFI calls expect."},"5587":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#current-status","content":"The FFI bridge exposes command-level operations for streams, topics, partitions, and segments; message sending and polling with user headers; consumer groups and consumer offsets; stats, client info, and cluster metadata; snapshots; user create/get/update/delete, permission updates and password changes; topic option discovery (describe_options); and raw binary requests (send_binary_request)."},"5588":{"id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/cpp/intro#current-status","content":"Not yet exposed: the high-level Producer/Consumer wrappers and personal access token management. Contributions are welcome."},"5589":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Go"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples"},"5590":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","content":"Runnable Go examples from the core repository, and how to start a server they can connect to."},"5591":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#starting-the-server","content":"Starting the server"},"5592":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Getting started"},"5593":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"TLS"},"5594":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","content":"A runnable getting-started example lives in the examples/go directory of the core repository. Its go.mod carries a replace directive pointing at the in-repo SDK, so the example always builds against the SDK source in the same checkout. The examples are exercised in CI via scripts/run-examples-from-readme.sh --language go."},"5595":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#starting-the-server","content":"The Go SDK speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. The examples require Go 1.25 or newer and log in as iggy/iggy. From the repository root, start a disposable development server with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides:"},"5596":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#starting-the-server","content":"--fresh wipes this replica's local data directory (local_data by default). Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. A fresh cluster replica can recover credentials from peers. This setup is intended only for development and testing."},"5597":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"The producer creates sample-stream and sample-topic (if missing) and sends 5 batches of 10 messages to partition 0. The consumer polls them from offset 0 and exits after 5 batches. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are 0-based. These repository examples use numeric stream and topic IDs 0, so create no other streams or topics before running the producer on the fresh server. Run the producer first."},"5598":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Run both from the examples/go directory:"},"5599":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Each consumer reads partition 0 independently, starting from offset 0 or the earliest retained message. Running several consumers reads the same messages; these examples do not join a consumer group."},"5600":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Both binaries accept the same flags:"},"5601":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"--tcp-server-address - server address, default 127.0.0.1:8090"},"5602":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"--tls - enable TLS"},"5603":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"--tls-ca-file - path to a CA certificate file"},"5604":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"--tls-domain - TLS server domain name for SNI"},"5605":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"From the repository root, start a disposable server with TLS enabled and the development certificates:"},"5606":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"The same data and credential prerequisites apply. These certificates are for development only. Run the producer before the consumer with the TLS flags (paths relative to examples/go):"},"5607":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"For the code walkthrough, see the quick start on the intro page, which follows the same example."},"5608":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"page","content":"Go SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Go"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro"},"5609":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","content":"The Go SDK, a blocking client speaking the VSR wire protocol over TCP, and which servers it pairs with."},"5610":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#server-compatibility","content":"Server compatibility"},"5611":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"5612":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"5613":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"5614":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"5615":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#tls","content":"TLS"},"5616":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#delivery-semantics","content":"Delivery semantics"},"5617":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups"},"5618":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"5619":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","content":"The Iggy Go SDK is a client library for interacting with the Iggy server from Go applications. It is a blocking client that speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol over TCP, with or without TLS. The package is available on pkg.go.dev and the source code lives in foreign/go."},"5620":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#server-compatibility","content":"The SDK and the server must speak the same wire protocol. Tagged releases up to v0.8.0 predate VSR (and the current API) and pair only with servers of the same era. The current SDK speaks VSR only. This page targets server 0.9.0; use a compatible SDK release or edge version. When working from source, build the SDK and the server from the same repository checkout."},"5621":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"The current source requires Go 1.25 or newer. Run these commands from your application module:"},"5622":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"To pin the release that matches server 0.9.0:"},"5623":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"For unreleased changes, use the local SDK replacement shown in the repository examples. A published edge tag need not contain every change in your checkout."},"5624":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"The snippets below follow the getting-started example. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are 0-based: the first partition of a topic is partition 0."},"5625":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"Run the server from the repository root with disposable development data and no root credential overrides:"},"5626":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"--fresh wipes this replica's local data directory. IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD take precedence over the flag; bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials, including credentials recovered from cluster peers. The snippets use iggy/iggy. Run the producer before the consumer."},"5627":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#producer","content":"CreateTopic accepts optional trailing iggcon.HeaderEntry values carrying named topic options, for option keys the server catalog gained after the SDK build shipped. DescribeOptions lists the keys a server accepts."},"5628":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer","content":"The consumer reads partition 0 independently and keeps its offset only in memory. Restarting reads from offset 0 again, or the earliest retained message if older messages were removed."},"5629":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer","content":"Uncompressed payloads and user headers alias the reply buffer, so retaining their slices pins the whole reply. Copy those bytes to retain only the message you need. S2 decompression allocates a separate payload buffer."},"5630":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#tls","content":"Replace the quick-start client constructor with the following TCP options. Start the TLS server as shown on the examples page; the development CA path below is relative to examples/go:"},"5631":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#tls","content":"tcp.WithTLSValidateCertificate controls certificate validation. The client also accepts client.WithLogger for a custom *slog.Logger and runs a built-in heartbeat loop (every 5 seconds by default) once connected."},"5632":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#delivery-semantics","content":"SendMessages returns any placements the server reports. A send whose reply is lost to a dropped connection returns ErrDisconnected without a replay: a reconnect registers a fresh client identity, so the server could not deduplicate the replay against a batch that may have already committed. Retrying such a send is the caller's decision and may write the batch twice. Consumers must handle duplicates through idempotent processing or application-level deduplication."},"5633":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#delivery-semantics","content":"Crash durability follows the topic's durability policy: replicated confirms replication, while persisted also waits for the required replicas to persist the message data. See Durability. An empty confirmation list is a valid success but does not by itself prove that new messages were appended."},"5634":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer-groups","content":"The client exposes the full consumer group API: CreateConsumerGroup, JoinConsumerGroup, LeaveConsumerGroup, GetConsumerGroups, and SyncConsumerGroup (which returns the partitions this client currently owns together with the group generation). To poll as a group member, build the consumer with iggcon.NewGroupConsumer and pass a nil partition id to PollMessages. The client orchestrates the group poll. An empty batch with no assigned partition means nothing to read right now (a member that owns no partition, or a rebalance still settling): poll again. ErrConsumerGroupMemberNotFound means the client is not a member, for example after an explicit LeaveConsumerGroup. JoinConsumerGroup restores membership."},"5635":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#examples","content":"A runnable getting-started example with producer, consumer, and TLS flags lives in examples/go. See the examples page for how to run it."},"5636":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","C#"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples"},"5637":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","content":"Producer and consumer samples for the C# SDK, built on the high-level publisher and consumer."},"5638":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#producer","content":"Producer"},"5639":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#consumer-group","content":"Consumer group"},"5640":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#typed-messages","content":"Typed messages"},"5641":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#more-examples","content":"More examples"},"5642":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","content":"These samples use the High-level SDK - the recommended way to build producers and consumers. For the low-level, per-call equivalents, see the Guide."},"5643":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","content":"Use the installation and server setup first. The snippets share their named resources: run a producer before its corresponding consumer."},"5644":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#producer","content":"A publisher that creates the stream and topic if missing, batches sends in the background, and retries failures:"},"5645":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#consumer-group","content":"A consumer that creates and joins a consumer group, stores each offset after handling the yielded message and requesting the next one, and surfaces polling errors:"},"5646":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#consumer-group","content":"Run several instances of the consumer to see the group load-balance partitions between members."},"5647":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#typed-messages","content":"A typed publisher/consumer pair that (de)serializes a record as JSON. The typed builders are configured via statements rather than one fluent chain - the With* methods return the untyped base builder, so Build() must be called on the typed builder variable (see Typed consumer):"},"5648":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#more-examples","content":"The examples/csharp directory in the Iggy repository contains complete, runnable projects:"},"5649":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#more-examples","content":"Basic / GettingStarted - low-level producer and consumer"},"5650":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#more-examples","content":"NewSdk - high-level IggyPublisher / IggyConsumer (like the samples above)"},"5651":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#more-examples","content":"MessageEnvelope - envelope pattern (message type + JSON payload) over the low-level client"},"5652":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples#more-examples","content":"MessageHeaders - user-defined message headers"},"5653":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/examples","tags":[],"