-142","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Observe polling errors"},"6042":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-143","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"WithLogger(loggerFactory)"},"6043":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-144","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Logger factory for diagnostics"},"6044":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-145","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"For consumer groups, use Next() with offset commits to track each partition independently. An Offset(...) strategy advances one shared cursor across the group's partitions and can skip messages from another partition."},"6045":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-146","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Auto-commit modes (AutoCommitMode):"},"6046":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-147","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Mode"},"6047":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-148","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Description"},"6048":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-149","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Auto"},"6049":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-150","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Advance/submit the offset during the server poll, before application processing"},"6050":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-151","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"AfterReceive"},"6051":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-152","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Store after the yielded message has been handled and enumeration resumes; breaking or throwing can leave that message uncommitted"},"6052":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-153","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Disabled"},"6053":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-154","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Commit manually"},"6054":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-155","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#manual-offset-control","content":"With AutoCommitMode.Disabled, commit offsets yourself via the consumer's StoreOffsetAsync:"},"6055":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-156","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#manual-offset-control","content":"StoreOffsetAsync(offset, partitionId, resetLastPolled = false) stores the offset for a partition. resetLastPolled: true also changes its duplicate-filter position; it does not change the configured polling strategy or remove buffered messages. DeleteOffsetAsync(partitionId) clears the server offset while preserving that local state. Create a new consumer to restart with a different position."},"6056":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-157","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-groups","content":"Pass a Consumer.Group(...) and let the builder create and join the group for load-balanced consumption:"},"6057":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-158","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consuming-with-rented-buffers","content":"On TCP, ReceiveAsync copies each payload into its own byte[]. ReceiveRentedAsync is the high-level counterpart of the low-level rented poll API: payloads are slices of a pooled buffer shared by all messages from the same poll. Each yielded ReceivedRentedMessage must be disposed - the buffer returns to the pool once the last message of its batch is disposed:"},"6058":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-159","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consuming-with-rented-buffers","content":"ReceivedRentedMessage (IDisposable) exposes Message (a RentedMessageResponse), CurrentOffset, PartitionId, Status, and Error. This path performs no deserialization, so Status is always Success. Payload and raw-header memory are only valid until the message is disposed - copy out anything you need to keep (e.g. message.Message.Payload.ToArray())."},"6059":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-160","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consuming-with-rented-buffers","content":"Auto-commit modes apply exactly as with ReceiveAsync. If a message is not disposed, the batch buffer is not returned to the pool; it becomes eligible for garbage collection once no references remain."},"6060":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-161","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-consumer","content":"For automatic deserialization, use IggyConsumerBuilder<T> with an IDeserializer<T> and iterate with ReceiveDeserializedAsync. Each ReceivedMessage<T> carries a Status you should check:"},"6061":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-162","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-consumer","content":"Note: On the typed builders, the fluent With* methods are inherited from the untyped base builder and return the base type, while the typed Build() hides the base one. Chaining Create(...).WithAutoCommitMode(...).Build() therefore resolves to the base Build() and returns an untyped consumer/publisher. Keep the typed builder in a variable (as above) and call Build() on it - the With* calls mutate the builder, so their return value can be ignored."},"6062":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"page","content":"C# SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","C#"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro"},"6063":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","content":"The async-first C# SDK for .NET applications over TCP and HTTP, from installation to creating a client."},"6064":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"6065":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"Supported protocols"},"6066":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Connection semantics"},"6067":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"Failed requests"},"6068":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"Creating a client"},"6069":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"6070":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"6071":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"6072":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"Next steps"},"6073":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","content":"The Iggy SDK for C# is a modern, async-first client library for interacting with an Iggy message streaming server from your .NET applications. It supports TCP and HTTP transports. The package is available on NuGet and the source code lives on GitHub."},"6074":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","content":"The SDK is built around the IIggyClient interface, which aggregates every feature (publishing, consuming, stream/topic management, users, offsets, consumer groups, and system operations). For the low-level per-call API and the full configuration reference, see the Guide. For the ergonomic, batteries-included producer/consumer abstractions, see the High-level SDK."},"6075":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"The SDK targets .NET 8 and .NET 10 (net8.0 and net10.0). Version 0.9.0 matches the server 0.9.0 protocol and includes both topic durability options. Version 0.8.0 uses the older TCP framing. When building from source, use the server and SDK from the same checkout."},"6076":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"To use the source SDK with these release-preparation examples, run from the Iggy checkout root:"},"6077":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"Put a sample in IggySample/Program.cs and run dotnet run --project IggySample. For console logging, add its package to that project:"},"6078":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"The SDK supports two transport protocols:"},"6079":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"TCP - binary protocol for optimal performance and lower latency (recommended). Over TCP the SDK speaks the VSR consensus framing, the only wire protocol the server accepts. See Connection semantics"},"6080":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"HTTP - RESTful JSON API for stateless operations. BaseAddress must then be a full URI such as http://127.0.0.1:3000 (the server's default HTTP port is 3000, TCP is 8090)"},"6081":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"Some operations are TCP-only and throw FeatureUnavailableException on HTTP: joining/leaving a consumer group, GetMeAsync, DeleteSegmentsAsync, and SendBinaryRequestAsync."},"6082":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Over TCP every request is wrapped in a 256-byte consensus header (Viewstamped Replication), the client registers a consensus session at login, and replicated writes complete after the required quorum acknowledges them. Topic Durability.Persisted additionally waits for local persistence on that quorum; message and explicit offset durability are configured independently. See Durability. Both transports implement IIggyClient, with these TCP connection rules:"},"6083":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Login binds a session. LoginUserAsync / LoginWithPersonalAccessTokenAsync run the register handshake, and the session lives for as long as the connection. Logging out, being evicted, or losing the connection ends it, and the next login registers a fresh one."},"6084":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Leader redirection is automatic. The client reads the cluster roster, follows the current leader, and re-checks it when a request is refused because the node stopped being primary. GetCurrentAddress() reports the node the client currently talks to."},"6085":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"The client picks partitions. Balanced and message-key partitioning are resolved client-side (identical encoded key bytes and partition counts produce the same xxHash32 routing result as the Rust SDK), and consumer-group polls round-robin over the partitions the coordinator assigned to this client."},"6086":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Consumer groups are assignment-based. JoinConsumerGroupAsync makes this client a member. The assignment is synced on demand and refreshed on every PingAsync. The TCP client pings on its own every HeartbeatInterval (5 seconds by default), so an idle session survives the server's heartbeat verification and assignments stay fresh."},"6087":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Credentials are bounds-checked locally. A username outside 3-50 bytes, a password outside 3-100 bytes, or a personal access token outside 1-255 bytes is rejected before the request is framed."},"6088":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Consumer offsets need an explicit partition. StoreOffsetAsync / DeleteOffsetAsync do not accept a null partition id under VSR. Passing one throws client-side."},"6089":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Polling a missing topic throws IggyInvalidStatusCodeException. An existing topic with no available messages returns an empty poll."},"6090":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"The SDK can replay a request the server reports as never admitted, within its request deadline. Replay-safe operations, including read-only polls, can also be retried after a lost connection. Two exceptions surface to the caller:"},"6091":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"IggyInvalidStatusCodeException carries the server status code, with FromServer telling apart a verdict the cluster reported from a failure the client raised itself."},"6092":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"VsrRequestOutcomeUnknownException means no definitive server verdict arrived for an operation that cannot safely be replayed after it was written (the connection was lost, the call was cancelled, or the server evicted the session mid-flight), so the cluster may or may not have committed it. The SDK will not replay it on a new session, because re-issuing a possibly committed operation can repeat its effects. Re-issuing it is the caller's decision."},"6093":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"The high-level abstractions handle the unknown-outcome case differently:"},"6094":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"IggyConsumer rethrows it rather than swallowing it, because an auto-committing poll may already have advanced the offset. Rethrowing ends the polling loop, so handle it around the enumeration. This fragment assumes an initialized consumer and a CancellationToken token, and stops consumption for an application-level recovery decision:"},"6095":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"IggyPublisher with background sending does not retry it: the batch is reported through the message-batch-failed event (SubscribeOnMessageBatchFailed), typed so a subscriber can tell \"not sent\" from \"possibly sent twice\". Direct (non-background) sends throw it to the caller."},"6096":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-33","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"The examples use iggy / iggy. For a new server data directory, start the matching source server from the Iggy repository root:"},"6097":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-34","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"For an existing server, use its configured credentials. Create a client with IggyClientFactory.CreateClient, then call ConnectAsync:"},"6098":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-35","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"Optionally, provide an ILoggerFactory for diagnostics (defaults to NullLoggerFactory.Instance). The console logger requires the Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console package:"},"6099":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-36","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"IggyClientConfigurator also exposes TCP socket buffers, TLS, heartbeat, response-frame limits, reconnection with exponential backoff (on by default), auto-login, and message encryption. HTTP clients require explicit login; the TCP connection settings are not applied to HTTP. See Client configuration for the full reference."},"6100":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-37","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#quick-start","content":"These samples use the High-level SDK - the recommended way to build producers and consumers. For the equivalent low-level, per-call flow, see the Guide."},"6101":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-38","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#consumer","content":"ReceiveAsync polls indefinitely - pass a CancellationToken or break out of the loop to stop."},"6102":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-39","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"Guide - client configuration reference and the full API surface: auth, streams, topics, partitions, publishing, consuming, offsets, consumer groups, system operations"},"6103":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-40","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"High-level SDK - IggyPublisher / IggyConsumer with background sending, retries, auto-commit, typed (de)serialization, and pooled (rented) buffers to avoid separate payload copies on TCP"},"6104":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-41","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"Examples - producer, consumer-group, and typed-message samples, plus links to runnable projects"},"6105":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Java"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples"},"6106":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","content":"Runnable Java examples from the core repository, built with Gradle, and how to start a server for them."},"6107":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#starting-the-server","content":"Starting the server"},"6108":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Running the examples"},"6109":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#blocking-vs-async","content":"Blocking vs. async"},"6110":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","content":"Runnable examples live in the examples/java directory of the core repository as a standalone Gradle project. The project builds against the in-repo SDK (via an includeBuild substitution), so the examples always match the SDK source in the same checkout. Java 17 or newer is required. The included gradlew wrapper downloads the pinned Gradle version on first run."},"6111":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#starting-the-server","content":"These examples target server 0.9.0 and speak the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. The examples log in as iggy/iggy. From the repository root, start a disposable development server with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides:"},"6112":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#starting-the-server","content":"--fresh wipes this replica's local data directory (local_data by default). Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. A fresh cluster replica can recover credentials from peers. This setup is intended only for development and testing."},"6113":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Run each example from the examples/java directory with its Gradle task. Run the producer before its consumer. The ordinary consumers keep their cursor in memory and start from offset 0 or the earliest retained message on each run; the multi-tenant consumer uses consumer groups and server-managed offsets."},"6114":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"gettingstarted - basic blocking producer and consumer, the best starting point."},"6115":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"messageheaders - message metadata via custom header keys and values, with a message_type header selecting the application handler."},"6116":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"messageenvelope - JSON envelope pattern for polymorphic message handling (order events wrapped in envelopes for type identification)."},"6117":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"multitenant - multi-tenant isolation: per-tenant streams, users with stream-specific permissions, and concurrent producers and consumers across tenants."},"6118":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"sinkdataproducer - high-volume data generation (100 batches of 1000 to 1099 messages) with realistic records, for testing and benchmarking."},"6119":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"streambuilder - a combined producer and consumer in a single class. It deletes its test_stream stream after the run."},"6120":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"async - the async client: CompletableFuture chaining, submitting multiple sends without blocking, backpressure, error recovery with exponential backoff, and thread pool separation."},"6121":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"tcptls - TLS-encrypted TCP connections with CA certificate verification. Requires a TLS-enabled server. Run this server command from the repository root:"},"6122":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The same data and credential prerequisites apply. These certificates are for development only. Return to examples/java for the client commands; their CA path is relative to that directory."},"6123":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#blocking-vs-async","content":"Use the blocking client for scripts, CLI tools, integration tests, and anywhere sequential code is easier to reason about. Use the async client for high throughput, reactive applications, and composing non-blocking requests with CompletableFuture. The async client runs I/O on Netty event loop threads: never block them with .join(), .get(), Thread.sleep(), or blocking I/O inside thenApply/thenAccept. Offload blocking work with thenApplyAsync(fn, executor). The examples/java README covers these async patterns in detail."},"6124":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"page","content":"Java SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Java"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro"},"6125":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","content":"The Java SDK, with blocking and CompletableFuture clients over TCP, and which servers they pair with."},"6126":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#server-compatibility","content":"Server compatibility"},"6127":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"6128":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#maven","content":"Maven"},"6129":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#gradle","content":"Gradle"},"6130":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"6131":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"6132":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"6133":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"Client types and configuration"},"6134":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"6135":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","content":"The Iggy Java SDK is a client library for interacting with the Iggy server from Java applications. It provides a blocking client and an async client (based on CompletableFuture) over TCP, plus a blocking HTTP client. The artifact is published to Maven Central and the source code lives in foreign/java."},"6136":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#server-compatibility","content":"This documentation targets server 0.9.0 and SDK 0.9.0. The TCP transport speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. The Java artifact 0.8.0 uses the older protocol. When working from source, build the SDK and the server from the same repository checkout."},"6137":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#installation","content":"Use Java 17 or newer. The SDK is published to Maven Central."},"6138":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#maven","content":"Add the dependency under the project element in pom.xml:"},"6139":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#gradle","content":"Check Maven Central for the latest published version. Snapshot builds are available from the ASF snapshot repository."},"6140":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#quick-start","content":"The snippets below follow the getting-started example. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are 0-based: the first partition of a topic is partition 0."},"6141":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#quick-start","content":"Start a development server using the example setup, with iggy/iggy credentials, then run the producer before the consumer. Bootstrap credentials do not replace recovered credentials."},"6142":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#producer","content":"createTopic also has an overload with a trailing Map<String, HeaderValue> of named topic options, for option keys the server catalog gained after the SDK build shipped."},"6143":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#consumer","content":"The consumer reads partition 0 independently and stops on an empty poll. Its cursor starts at offset 0 or the earliest retained message each time it runs; autoCommit is disabled, so this sample does not store its progress on the server."},"6144":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"org.apache.iggy.Iggy is the unified entrypoint for building clients. Iggy.tcpClientBuilder().blocking() returns the same builder as IggyTcpClient.builder() used above."},"6145":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"Close clients when finished: client.close(), asyncClient.close().join(), and httpClient.close(). The HTTP close method declares IOException."},"6146":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"The TCP builder also accepts TLS and resilience options. Start the TLS development server and run this fragment with examples/java as the working directory:"},"6147":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"Beyond streams, topics, and messages, the client exposes consumer groups, partitions, users, and personal access tokens through client.consumerGroups(), client.partitions(), client.users(), and client.personalAccessTokens(). The SDK's custom exception types inherit from IggyException; failed futures can wrap them in CompletionException when joined."},"6148":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#examples","content":"Runnable examples covering the async client, message headers and envelopes, multi-tenant setups, and TLS live in examples/java. See the examples page for how to run them."},"6149":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Node"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples"},"6150":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"Build the local Node.js SDK and run its TypeScript examples."},"6151":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Running the examples"},"6152":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"Examples are available in the examples/node directory, written in TypeScript. They use the SDK from the same checkout:"},"6153":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"getting-started - basic producer and consumer"},"6154":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"basic - producer and consumer with utilities"},"6155":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"message-envelope - JSON message envelope pattern"},"6156":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"message-headers - message-type dispatch using a JSON wrapper in the payload"},"6157":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"multi-tenant - separate streams for tenants, using one client"},"6158":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"tcp-tls - TLS-encrypted TCP connections"},"6159":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"stream-builder - end-to-end walkthrough: create a stream and topic, produce, and consume"},"6160":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"sink-data-producer - bulk data generation for sink connectors"},"6161":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"These examples target server 0.9.0. See Getting started for prerequisites:"},"6162":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The default-root flag sets the iggy/iggy credentials used by the examples. --fresh deletes existing local server data. Explicit credential environment variables override the default-root flag; bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials."},"6163":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"From the repository root, build the local SDK before installing the examples. The stream-builder example creates a stream and topic, sends and consumes three messages, and deletes its resources:"},"6164":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Each example has a script in examples/node/package.json. Set DEBUG=iggy:examples* to see its progress."},"6165":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The separate producer and consumer scripts do not consistently share resources: the envelope and headers consumers create new empty topics, and the tenant producer deletes its streams before exiting. The getting-started and basic producers spread batches over five partitions, while their consumers poll partition 0. Use the self-contained stream-builder command above for a complete send-and-consume walkthrough. The sink-data producer deletes its selected topic and stream when it finishes, including resources that already existed."},"6166":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The TCP/TLS pair needs a TLS-enabled source server. Run this command from the repository root, then the two test:tcp-tls:* scripts from examples/node, where they load ../../core/certs/iggy_ca_cert.pem:"},"6167":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"These are repository development certificates."},"6168":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"For the quick-start code itself, see the Node.js SDK intro."},"6169":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"page","content":"Node.js SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Node"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro"},"6170":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","content":"The Node.js and TypeScript SDK, from installation to keeping client and server versions in step."},"6171":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"6172":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"6173":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"6174":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"6175":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer-group","content":"Consumer group"},"6176":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"Running the samples again"},"6177":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"Configuration"},"6178":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Beyond the basics"},"6179":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"6180":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","content":"The Iggy Node.js SDK is a client library that allows you to interact with the Iggy API from your Node.js and TypeScript applications. It communicates with the Iggy server over TCP or TLS using the binary protocol. The package is available on npm and the source code can be found on GitHub."},"6181":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","content":"These docs target server 0.9.0 and Node SDK 0.10.0. The Node package 0.8.0 uses the older protocol. The current SDK speaks only the VSR wire protocol and doesn't fall back to older formats."},"6182":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"The samples below expect an Iggy server on 127.0.0.1:8090. See Getting started for the server prerequisites:"},"6183":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and set the root credentials the samples log in with. Without --with-default-root-credentials (or the IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME / IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD variables), a first boot generates a random root password instead of iggy/iggy. For source runs, --fresh deletes existing local server data. Explicit credential environment variables take precedence over the default-root flag; bootstrap settings do not replace credentials already stored in recovered data."},"6184":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"Save the following snippets as producer.mjs and consumer.mjs in the project where you installed the SDK. Run node producer.mjs before node consumer.mjs. A consumer group sample follows them."},"6185":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#producer","content":"The Node SDK sends the partitioning choice on the wire, and the server resolves balanced and key routing at admission (the Rust, C#, Java, and Go binary clients instead resolve them client-side)."},"6186":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer","content":"The poll response does not wait for the auto-commit to finish. The sample processes messages after polling, so auto-commit does not confirm that the application handled each message."},"6187":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer-group","content":"A consumer group lets several consumers share a topic. The server assigns partitions to the members and stores the group's offset. Save this as consumer-group.mjs:"},"6188":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer-group","content":"The group's offset is separate from the single consumer's, so the first run of node consumer-group.mjs reads the topic from offset 0 even after consumer.mjs has run."},"6189":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"All three samples can be run again without changes:"},"6190":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"node producer.mjs finds the existing stream and topic and appends ten more messages."},"6191":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"node consumer.mjs prints only the new messages, because PollingStrategy.Next continues after the stored offset. With nothing new to read, it prints nothing."},"6192":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"node consumer-group.mjs does the same from the group's stored offset."},"6193":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"To start again with an empty server, stop the container and run the docker run command again. --rm removes the container and its data."},"6194":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"The client constructor accepts a few options:"},"6195":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"reconnect: automatic reconnection with { enabled, interval, maxRetries }."},"6196":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"heartbeatInterval: the client pings the server every 5 seconds by default. 0 disables heartbeats. When server heartbeat eviction is enabled, silent consumer-group members can be evicted, so keep it well below the server's heartbeat timeout."},"6197":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"maxResponseFrameSize: response frames larger than this limit (default 64 MiB) are rejected and close the connection. Raise it when polling very large batches."},"6198":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"poolSize: the client currently supports exactly one pooled connection. min/max above 1 throw."},"6199":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"TLS: set transport: 'TLS' and pass Node tls.ConnectionOptions in options. See the tcp-tls example."},"6200":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Consumer groups: client.group manages groups (including ensure-and-join in one call), and polling with Consumer.Group(groupId) and partitionId: null follows the partitions assigned to that member."},"6201":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Consumer streams: singleConsumerStream and groupConsumerStream expose polling as Node streams."},"6202":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Administration: users, personal access tokens, offsets, partitions, segments, and cluster metadata are all available on the client."},"6203":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-33","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Errors: the package exports ResponseError, DeserializeError, ProtocolFrameError, and VsrEvictionError for precise error handling."},"6204":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-34","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"See the foreign/node README for the full API walkthrough."},"6205":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-35","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#examples","content":"Examples are available in the examples/node directory, written in TypeScript. See Examples for the list and how to run them."},"6206":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"page","content":"PHP SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","PHP"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro"},"6207":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","content":"The experimental PHP SDK, a native extension wrapping the Rust SDK, and how to build it from source."},"6208":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"Requirements"},"6209":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#build-and-install","content":"Build and install"},"6210":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"6211":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups"},"6212":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"API notes"},"6213":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#current-status","content":"Current status"},"6214":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"6215":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","content":"The Iggy PHP SDK is a native PHP extension built in Rust with ext-php-rs. It wraps the Rust SDK and exposes a synchronous Iggy\\Client: each call drives the extension's internal Tokio runtime and blocks the calling PHP thread until the operation completes. The source code is available on GitHub."},"6216":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","content":"The extension is experimental. It's defined as the apache/iggy-php composer package (php-ext type). To use it today, build it from the repository source."},"6217":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"Rust and Cargo"},"6218":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"PHP 8.3 or newer with php-config (non-thread-safe builds only, no ZTS)"},"6219":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"cargo-php"},"6220":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#build-and-install","content":"Alternatively, skip the install and point PHP at the built library per invocation:"},"6221":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#build-and-install","content":"On macOS the library is libiggy_php.dylib."},"6222":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"The sample below targets Iggy 0.9.0 on 127.0.0.1:8090. Start the server:"},"6223":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and bootstrap the iggy/iggy root credentials for a new data directory. Existing stored credentials are not replaced. For the source command, ensure IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD are unset or match the sample; environment values take precedence over the flag. --fresh removes the local replica state, so use it only for disposable development data."},"6224":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"For binary transports, credentials in the connection string take effect on connect(). HTTP requires loginUser() explicitly. Alternatively, construct the client with a plain address (new \\Iggy\\Client('127.0.0.1:8090')) and call loginUser('iggy', 'iggy') explicitly."},"6225":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"consumerGroup() returns an Iggy\\Consumer that creates and joins the group by default. The partition argument is ignored for consumer groups; the server assigns partitions. After the quickstart has sent ten messages, these two loops consume five each. Both wait for messages until their limit is reached:"},"6226":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"AutoCommit::when() may queue an offset commit before the callback runs. To gate commits on the callback succeeding, use AutoCommit::disabled() and call storeOffset() after the work succeeds, as above."},"6227":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Methods are camelCase and classes live in the Iggy namespace, for example Iggy\\Client and Iggy\\SendMessage."},"6228":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Partition ids are 0-based: for a topic with one partition, use 0."},"6229":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"PHP strings are passed as named identifiers, including strings that contain only digits. PHP integers are passed as numeric identifiers."},"6230":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"PollingStrategy::timestamp() and timestampMicros() expect microseconds since the Unix epoch. Use PollingStrategy::timestampSeconds() for PHP time() values."},"6231":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Message IDs and checksums are returned as decimal strings. Offset and timestamp getters return PHP integers and are limited to PHP_INT_MAX."},"6232":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Errors are thrown as exceptions under Iggy\\Exception: AuthenticationException, ConnectionException, NotFoundException, TransientException, and the IggyException base."},"6233":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"TLS uses the Rust SDK connection-string format, for example iggy+tcp://user:pass@host:port?tls=true&tls_domain=localhost&tls_ca_file=/path/to/ca.pem."},"6234":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"The extension owns a lazy global Tokio runtime. Don't call pcntl_fork() after the first Iggy call: the child inherits file descriptors but not the runtime's worker threads."},"6235":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#current-status","content":"Iggy\\Client currently exposes stream and topic create/get/delete, message sending and polling, consumer groups with offset management, ping(), loginUser(), and raw binary requests (sendBinaryRequest()). Other administration surfaces (users, permissions, personal access tokens, partitions) aren't exposed yet."},"6236":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/php directory. They read IGGY_CONNECTION_STRING for a binary transport with credentials. Otherwise they connect over TCP using IGGY_HOST and IGGY_PORT, then log in with the literal IGGY_USERNAME and IGGY_PASSWORD values:"},"6237":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Python"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples"},"6238":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"Working Python examples from the core repository, covering connection strings, message headers and TLS."},"6239":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Running the examples"},"6240":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/python directory. CI runs the source examples against a source-built server, including a separate TLS pass:"},"6241":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"getting-started - producer and consumer with optional TLS via TcpConfig"},"6242":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"basic - producer and consumer using connection strings"},"6243":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"message-headers - user headers with str, bytes, bool, int, and float values, in two variants: plain-headers (plain dict, the SDK infers wire types) and typed-headers (explicit HeaderKey/HeaderValue)"},"6244":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"These examples target Iggy 0.9.0. Start the server:"},"6245":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and bootstrap the iggy/iggy root credentials for new data. Stored credentials are not replaced, and environment credentials override the source command's default-credentials flag. Use --fresh only with disposable local replica data."},"6246":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"With Python 3.10 or newer and Rust/Cargo available, run from examples/python. uv uses the local SDK path in pyproject.toml; the pip command below names that path explicitly:"},"6247":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"For the quick-start code itself, see the Python SDK intro."},"6248":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"page","content":"Python SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Python"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro"},"6249":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","content":"The Python SDK, a PyO3 wrapper around the Rust SDK, and how to install it."},"6250":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"6251":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"6252":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"6253":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"6254":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Beyond the basics"},"6255":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"6256":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","content":"The Iggy Python SDK is a client library that allows you to interact with the Iggy API from your Python application. It is built as a PyO3 wrapper around the Rust SDK, which means it supports TCP, QUIC, HTTP, and WebSocket transports via connection strings. The package is available on PyPI and the source code can be found on GitHub."},"6257":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","content":"The wheel bundles the Rust SDK, whose binary transports speak the current Iggy wire protocol without falling back to older formats. Use an SDK release compatible with your server. For unreleased changes, build the SDK and server from the same source checkout; the published wheel can lag source API changes."},"6258":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#installation","content":"Python 3.10 or newer is required. Install the published package in a virtual environment:"},"6259":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#installation","content":"For the source version described here, install from the repository root with Rust and Cargo available:"},"6260":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#quick-start","content":"The samples below target Iggy 0.9.0 on 127.0.0.1:8090:"},"6261":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#quick-start","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and bootstrap the iggy/iggy root credentials for new data. Stored credentials are not replaced. Environment credentials override the default-credentials flag, so unset them or set them to match the sample. --fresh deletes local replica state; use disposable development data. A new standalone server without explicit credentials generates a root password; a new cluster requires explicit credentials."},"6262":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"TLS: use TcpConfig with tls_enabled and tls_ca_file, or the TLS options in a connection string. The certificate must match tls_domain, which defaults to the dialed host. The getting-started example shows the full setup."},"6263":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Consumer groups: await client.consumer_group(...) returns an IggyConsumer that creates and joins the group by default and commits offsets according to the configured AutoCommit mode. Groups require a binary transport; HTTP supports ordinary consumers only."},"6264":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"User headers: SendMessage(data, user_headers=...) accepts a plain dict with str, bytes, bool, int, or float values. HeaderKey/HeaderValue give explicit control over the wire type. See the message-headers examples."},"6265":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Topic options: create_topic(..., options=...) accepts extra option keys as a dict[str, str], validated against the server's catalog. client.describe_options(\"topic\") lists the keys a server accepts."},"6266":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Administration: user and permission management. Personal access tokens can be used for login via AutoLogin.personal_access_token(...) in the connection config. PAT management isn't exposed yet."},"6267":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/python directory. See Examples for the list and how to run them."},"6268":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples"},"6269":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"The Rust SDK examples in the core repository, and how to start a server to run them against."},"6270":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"In the core repository, you can find the following examples using the Rust SDK."},"6271":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. Start the server from the repository root in a separate terminal. The examples use iggy/iggy; for a new local instance with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides:"},"6272":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. --fresh deletes this replica's local data; use disposable development data. A new standalone server without explicit credentials generates a root password; a new cluster requires explicit credentials."},"6273":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"The examples are cargo example targets of the iggy_examples crate. Run them from the repository root with cargo run --example <name> so relative certificate paths resolve. Run each producer before its consumer. Some examples create fixed names and require fresh data when repeated; the getting-started consumer also expects stream and topic IDs 0, so run that pair first on a fresh server. Partition IDs are 0-based, but balanced producers and consumer groups can use multiple partitions."},"6274":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Getting started - the basic example which is discussed in the getting started guide."},"6275":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Basic - producer and consumer using the low-level Client trait with CLI args for transport selection (TCP/QUIC/HTTP/WebSocket)."},"6276":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"New SDK - an introduction to the high-level SDK as discussed in the dedicated guide."},"6277":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Stream builder - examples using the IggyStream, IggyStreamProducer, and IggyStreamConsumer builder APIs as discussed in the stream builder guide."},"6278":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Message envelope - the example of how to send a message with a custom envelope e.g. to differentiate between different types of messages."},"6279":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Message headers - the example of how to send a message with custom headers (typed headers, message type discrimination, client-side compression)."},"6280":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Multi-tenant - the larger example of how to structure your application to support multiple tenants (separated by the unique streams) with the different users, permissions, topics etc."},"6281":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"TCP TLS - TLS-encrypted TCP connections with custom CA certificates. Stop the plain server and start a TLS-enabled server from the repository root:"},"6282":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"These certificates are for local testing. Then run:"},"6283":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Sink data producer - generating random JSON records in bulk batches for sink connector testing."},"6284":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"page","content":"High-level SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk"},"6285":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"IggyProducer and IggyConsumer: batching, consumer groups and offset commits without the boilerplate."},"6286":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Connection string"},"6287":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Producer"},"6288":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Background producer configuration"},"6289":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#send-confirmations","content":"Send confirmations"},"6290":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"6291":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Consumer builder options"},"6292":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"Auto-commit matrix"},"6293":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#raw-requests","content":"Raw requests"},"6294":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"If you've read through the getting started guide, you might have noticed that it's quite verbose and requires a lot of boilerplate code to get started. This is where the High-level SDK comes in, as it does provide a more user-friendly interface to interact with the Iggy API for both, producer and consumer. Let's consider the following features:"},"6295":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"Automatically creating & joining the consumer groups"},"6296":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"Committing the offset depending on the particular mode (e.g. in the background based on some interval, when an offset matches the configured trigger etc.)"},"6297":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"Batching the messages, whether it's about producing or consuming"},"6298":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"Processing the messages as if the stream was an async iterator"},"6299":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"Reusing the same client for both, producing and consuming on the same topic without repeating the configuration"},"6300":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"And more..."},"6301":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"The Rust snippets assume an async function returning Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>, with iggy, Tokio and futures-util dependencies. Use the Rust SDK setup and a server with the sample credentials."},"6302":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Instead of providing the configuration for the client, you can use the connection string. It's a string that contains all the necessary information to connect to the Iggy API, and it works with all four transports:"},"6303":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Which can be used to create the client like this:"},"6304":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"HTTP requires an explicit login after construction. Consumer groups require a binary transport; use an ordinary consumer with an explicit partition over HTTP."},"6305":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Options are appended as query parameters. For TCP:"},"6306":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"The option keys differ per transport (for example QUIC uses reconnection_max_retries and reconnection_reestablish_after where TCP uses reconnection_retries and reestablish_after). Unknown keys are rejected as hard errors. See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables and defaults."},"6307":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Unless you need client-side encryption, a custom Partitioner, or other settings outside the connection-string options, you should be good to go with the connection string. On the other hand, you can always make use of IggyClientBuilder::from_connection_string() to extend the options on top of the provided connection string."},"6308":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"The IggyClient internally wraps its transport in an IggyRwLock, but the client itself does not implement Clone. Share an Arc<IggyClient> across tasks; cloning the Arc keeps the same client and connection. Typically, it's a good idea to create a separate connection for producing and consuming, but it's not a requirement."},"6309":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"The producer is a high-level abstraction that allows you to send messages to the topic. It's quite simple to use and doesn't require you to handle the offsets, partitions or any other low-level details. To begin with, you can simply invoke client.producer() to get the IggyProducerBuilder allowing you to configure the producer. Let's take a look at the basic example:"},"6310":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"The direct producer splits each send() call into requests of at most 1000 messages. For sequential calls, the 5 ms linger setting delays a call until at least 5 ms after the previous successful send; it does not buffer messages between calls or delay individual chunks within a call. Choose background to buffer sends on worker tasks. balanced routes requests round-robin across partitions. By default, init() creates a missing stream and topic, requiring the corresponding permissions."},"6311":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Finally, you can use the send() method to send the messages to the topic. The producer never needs to be a mutable binding: both init() and send() take &self, so a plain let producer is enough for its whole life. Here's how you can send the messages:"},"6312":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Of course, you can provide the message headers, custom binary serialization etc. as it's the same IggyMessage type as the one used in the low-level API (use IggyMessage::builder() to set headers or a custom ID). The producer will take care of the rest, including the retries, partitioning, batching etc."},"6313":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Besides send(), the producer also offers send_one(message), send_with_partitioning(messages, partitioning) to override the partitioning per call, and send_to(stream, topic, messages, partitioning) to target a different stream and topic."},"6314":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"The background mode buffers messages and sends them from shard workers, trading latency for throughput and backpressure control. BackgroundConfig::builder() exposes:"},"6315":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Field"},"6316":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Default"},"6317":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Description"},"6318":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-33","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"num_shards"},"6319":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-34","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"1"},"6320":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-35","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Number of shard workers running in parallel"},"6321":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-36","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"sharding"},"6322":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-37","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"OrderedSharding"},"6323":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-38","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Routes each send to a worker. OrderedSharding keeps a stream/topic pair on one sequential worker; BalancedSharding round-robins across workers for maximum throughput and can reorder sends"},"6324":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-39","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"linger_time"},"6325":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-40","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"1ms"},"6326":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-41","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"How long a shard may wait before flushing an incomplete batch"},"6327":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-42","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"batch_size"},"6328":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-43","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"1 MiB"},"6329":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-44","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Per-worker flush threshold in bytes; 0 disables this trigger"},"6330":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-45","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"batch_length"},"6331":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-46","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"1000"},"6332":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-47","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Per-worker flush threshold in queued sends, not individual messages; 0 disables this trigger"},"6333":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-48","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"failure_mode"},"6334":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-49","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Block"},"6335":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-50","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Behavior when the byte budget is exhausted: Block, BlockWithTimeout(duration), or FailImmediately"},"6336":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-51","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"max_buffer_size"},"6337":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-52","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"32 MiB"},"6338":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-53","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Budget for message bytes buffered or in flight across all workers; 0 disables this budget"},"6339":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-54","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"max_in_flight"},"6340":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-55","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"1"},"6341":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-56","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Maximum concurrent writes across workers; each worker remains sequential. 0 uses the semaphore's maximum capacity"},"6342":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-57","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"error_callback"},"6343":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-58","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"LogErrorCallback"},"6344":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-59","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Async callback invoked on errors the producer cannot recover from"},"6345":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-60","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"batch_size and batch_length are flush triggers checked after adding a send. The final send can take the buffer past the byte threshold. The linger deadline starts when an empty buffer receives a send; the first trigger reached causes a flush. Each worker also has a bounded queue, which can make dispatch wait independently of failure_mode. A send larger than the entire byte budget fails under every backpressure mode. Nonzero max_buffer_size and max_in_flight values must not exceed tokio::sync::Semaphore::MAX_PERMITS; constructing the dispatcher with a larger value panics."},"6346":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-61","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Stop all senders, then call producer.shutdown().await to drain background queues and wait for writes and error callbacks. Dropping the producer without shutdown can lose buffered messages. Shutdown does not turn failed writes into successful ones; the default error callback logs and drops failed sends."},"6347":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-62","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#send-confirmations","content":"Every send method returns a SendMessagesResponse carrying the commit confirmations of the chunks the send was split into. The list is empty whenever the server sends no confirmation payload, and always for a background producer, which hands the messages to a dispatcher and returns before the send happens. Branch on confirmations.is_empty() instead of indexing. A retried chunk may already have been committed by an earlier attempt, so a confirmation offset does not imply uniqueness. Direct-send completion follows the topic's durability policy."},"6348":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-63","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer","content":"The consumer is a high-level abstraction that allows you to receive the messages from the topic. It's quite simple to use and doesn't require you to handle the offsets, partitions or any other low-level details. To begin with, you can simply invoke client.consumer() to get the IggyConsumerBuilder allowing you to configure the consumer. Let's take a look at the basic example:"},"6349":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-64","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer","content":"The consumer polls at most 1000 messages per request with a minimum 1 ms gap between polls. It requests offset commits every second or when the current message buffer becomes empty; these triggers do not observe application processing. The polling strategy is set to next which means that the consumer will try to consume the next available message from the partition currently assigned to the consumer group (you can also invoke a regular consumer() builder if you do not plan to use the consumer groups). The build() method is used to create the consumer."},"6350":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-65","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer","content":"Finally, you can use the next() method to receive the messages from the topic. Unlike the producer, the consumer must stay a mutable binding for its whole life: init() takes &mut self, and next() comes from the futures Stream implementation which also requires mutable access. The init() is used to ensure that the consumer is ready to receive the messages by validating the existence of the stream, topic, consumer group etc. Here's how you can consume the messages:"},"6351":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-66","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer","content":"In order to use the async iterator extension, add futures-util dependency and import futures_util::StreamExt."},"6352":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-67","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Defaults and lesser-known knobs on IggyConsumerBuilder:"},"6353":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-68","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Option"},"6354":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-69","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Default"},"6355":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-70","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Description"},"6356":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-71","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"polling_strategy(...)"},"6357":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-72","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"next()"},"6358":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-73","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Where to start polling"},"6359":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-74","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"batch_length(n)"},"6360":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-75","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"1000"},"6361":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-76","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Maximum messages polled per request"},"6362":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-77","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"auto_commit(...)"},"6363":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-78","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"IntervalOrWhen(1s, PollingMessages)"},"6364":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-79","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"See the matrix below"},"6365":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-80","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"auto_join_consumer_group()"},"6366":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-81","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"enabled"},"6367":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-82","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Join the consumer group automatically"},"6368":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-83","page