"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"init_retries(retries, interval)"},"6378":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-93","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"disabled"},"6379":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-94","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Retry init() when the stream or topic does not exist yet (e.g. created dynamically by a producer)"},"6380":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-95","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"allow_replay()"},"6381":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-96","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"disabled"},"6382":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-97","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Allow re-consuming messages at or below the stored offset"},"6383":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-98","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"offset_drain_timeout(d)"},"6384":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-99","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"5s"},"6385":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-100","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"How long shutdown() waits for background auto-commit tasks to drain before leaving the group"},"6386":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-101","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"encryptor(...)"},"6387":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-102","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"inherited from the client; none by default"},"6388":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-103","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Client-side payload and user-header decryption"},"6389":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-104","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"With an encryptor, init() rejects the PollingMessages auto-commit trigger because a batch might fail decryption after its offset was committed. Use a compatible mode, such as AutoCommit::Disabled, and store offsets explicitly after successful processing."},"6390":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-105","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"AutoCommit decides when the consumer requests offset storage:"},"6391":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-106","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"Variant"},"6392":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-107","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"Commit trigger"},"6393":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-108","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"Disabled"},"6394":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-109","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"Never automatically; store it manually"},"6395":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-110","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"Interval(d)"},"6396":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-111","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"Every d in the background"},"6397":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-112","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"IntervalOrWhen(d, when)"},"6398":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-113","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"Every d, plus at the when trigger"},"6399":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-114","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"IntervalOrAfter(d, after)"},"6400":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-115","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"Every d, plus at the after trigger"},"6401":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-116","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"When(when)"},"6402":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-117","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"At the when trigger"},"6403":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-118","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"After(after)"},"6404":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-119","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"At the after trigger"},"6405":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-120","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"AutoCommitWhen triggers fire while receiving: PollingMessages accompanies the poll request, ConsumingEachMessage runs before yielding a message, and ConsumingAllMessages runs when the current buffer is empty. ConsumingEveryNthMessage(n) checks whether the message offset is divisible by n, rather than counting processed messages; n = 0 disables that trigger."},"6406":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-121","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"AutoCommitAfter triggers run after the handler returns, including when it returns an error. ConsumingEachMessage and ConsumingEveryNthMessage(n) use the handled message's offset. ConsumingAllMessages fires when that offset reaches the partition head reported by the poll, so a lagging consumer may process several batches before it fires. For commits only after successful processing, disable auto-commit and store offsets explicitly."},"6407":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-122","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"The After triggers require consume_messages() from the IggyConsumerMessageExt trait (see Stream Builder). The plain next() loop cannot observe when processing finished, though IntervalOrAfter still commits on its interval. Background commits can fail; use an explicit offset-store result when completion must be confirmed."},"6408":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-123","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#raw-requests","content":"For custom commands the IggyClient exposes two escape hatches below the typed API:"},"6409":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-124","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#raw-requests","content":"send_binary_request(code, payload) sends a raw binary command and returns the raw response. Binary transports only. On HTTP it returns FeatureUnavailable. Login, registration and logout codes are rejected with InvalidCommand, use login_user/logout_user so the SDK session state stays correct. Custom codes are forwarded to the server, which decides whether it implements them."},"6410":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-125","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#raw-requests","content":"send_http_request(method, path, body) invokes an arbitrary HTTP endpoint and returns the raw response body. HTTP transport only. Binary transports return FeatureUnavailable."},"6411":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"page","content":"Intro","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro"},"6412":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","content":"The Rust SDK, the primary and most complete Iggy client, and where its source lives."},"6413":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"6414":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"High-level vs low-level API"},"6415":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"Connection string"},"6416":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#quick-example","content":"Quick example"},"6417":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","content":"The Rust SDK is the primary and most feature-complete client for Iggy. It is available on crates.io and the source code is part of the core repository."},"6418":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","content":"Run from your application crate to add the published SDK:"},"6419":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","content":"Use an SDK release compatible with your server. For unreleased changes, build both from the same checkout; the Getting Started guide shows the source dependency setup."},"6420":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#prerequisites","content":"The examples below log in with iggy/iggy. For a new local development instance, with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides, use:"},"6421":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#prerequisites","content":"--fresh wipes this replica's local data directory. Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. In a cluster, a fresh replica can recover credentials from peers. Use disposable development data. A new standalone server without explicit credentials generates a root password; a new cluster requires explicit credentials."},"6422":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"The SDK provides two layers. The snippets below assume an async Tokio context with a Result return type."},"6423":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"High-level API (recommended) - the easiest way to get started. It handles connection management, auto-batching, consumer group lifecycle, offset commits, retry logic, and reconnection out of the box. Use IggyClient, IggyProducer, and IggyConsumer for the best developer experience."},"6424":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"Low-level API - direct access to individual protocol operations. Useful when you need fine-grained control over every request, or when building custom abstractions on top."},"6425":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"The high-level API is built on top of the low-level one, so you can always drop down when needed."},"6426":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"The connection string is the simplest way to configure the client. It supports all four transport protocols:"},"6427":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket apply connection-string credentials on connect(). HTTP requires an explicit login and supports ordinary consumers only."},"6428":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"Options can be appended as query parameters:"},"6429":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"Personal Access Tokens are also supported. Any credential without a colon is treated as a PAT. Paste the token exactly as the server returned it, with no prefix:"},"6430":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables and defaults."},"6431":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#quick-example","content":"Partition IDs are 0-based: the first partition of a topic is partition 0. The consumer uses the default consumer identity and requests auto-commit; its offset can advance before application processing finishes. Use explicit offset storage after successful processing when that distinction matters."},"6432":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#quick-example","content":"For the full getting started tutorial, see Getting Started. For the high-level producer/consumer builders, see High-level SDK and Stream Builder."},"6433":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"page","content":"Stream Builder","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder"},"6434":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"The stream builder, for event-driven applications that would otherwise repeat producer and consumer setup."},"6435":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#iggystream-builder","content":"IggyStream Builder"},"6436":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#iggystreamproducer-builder","content":"IggyStreamProducer Builder"},"6437":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#producer-configuration","content":"Producer configuration"},"6438":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#iggystreamconsumer-builder","content":"IggyStreamConsumer Builder"},"6439":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-consumer-and-client","content":"Build consumer and client"},"6440":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-consumer-from-an-existing-client","content":"Build consumer from an existing client"},"6441":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#consumer-configuration","content":"Consumer configuration"},"6442":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#add-consumers-dynamically-at-runtime","content":"Add consumers dynamically at runtime."},"6443":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#add-producers-dynamically-at-runtime","content":"Add producers dynamically at runtime."},"6444":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"In the previous section, the high level SDK introduced the connection string to simplify the client configuration and\nindeed, it does provide a simple way to connect to the server. However, when you write an event based application,\nyou typically encounter one or more of the following scenarios:"},"6445":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"The consumer and producer operate on the same stream and topic."},"6446":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"The consumer operates on different streams and topics."},"6447":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"The producers operate on different streams and topics."},"6448":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"Add consumers dynamically at runtime."},"6449":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"Add producers dynamically at runtime."},"6450":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"The stream builder provides a convenient way to create the iggy client, producer and consumer for these use cases.\nAll source code examples are located in the examples folder of the iggy repository. Also,\nif you encounter a problem with any of the examples below, please ask in the community discord."},"6451":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"The examples below use iggy/iggy. For a new local development instance, with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides, use:"},"6452":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. --fresh deletes this replica's local data; use disposable development data. A new standalone server without explicit credentials generates a root password; a new cluster requires explicit credentials."},"6453":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"Use the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. The examples import PrintEventConsumer from the local iggy_examples crate and can run as the targets in examples/rust. In your own application, use the implementation shown below instead of that import."},"6454":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#iggystream-builder","content":"In the first case, the IggyStream Builder offers a fast and efficient way to get you started:"},"6455":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#iggystream-builder","content":"As you can see, the IggyStream builder is used to create the iggy client, producer and consumer.\nHere, we use the default configuration to get started quickly, but you can always customize the configuration\n(IggyStreamConfig::from_stream_topic or the two per-side configs) to fit your requirements. If you already have a\nconnected client, use IggyStream::build(&client, &stream_config) instead, which returns just the producer and consumer."},"6456":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#iggystream-builder","content":"The consumer is bound as mut: consume_messages (and the underlying message stream) requires mutable access."},"6457":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#iggystream-builder","content":"The consume_messages method is not part of the IggyConsumer. It comes from the IggyConsumerMessageExt\ntrait, which is re-exported by iggy::prelude. The PrintEventConsumer implements the MessageConsumer trait\nand is invoked to process each incoming message. A simple implementation is shown below:"},"6458":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#iggystreamproducer-builder","content":"When you implement the producer side, you can use the IggyStreamProducer to get started:"},"6459":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#iggystreamproducer-builder","content":"If you already have a custom iggy client, you can use that one by replacing the with_client_from_url\nconstructor with the following:"},"6460":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#iggystreamproducer-builder","content":"You find sample utils to build a customized iggy client in\nthe examples folder of the iggy\nrepository."},"6461":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#producer-configuration","content":"The IggyProducerConfig gives you a way to configure the producer in sufficient detail. Please note, if you have\nquestions about any of those settings, please ask in the community discord. For basic customization, the from_stream_topic constructor\nlets you set a custom stream and topic name as well as the maximum batch length and linger time between sends. The producer resolves destinations by name; keep the corresponding identifier fields consistent with those names."},"6462":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#producer-configuration","content":"The remaining configuration fields are set to default values applicable to the most common use case. This should be sufficient for a simple application, or proof of concept without getting lost in details.\nHowever, for more complex applications, you might want to configure more\ndetails and for that, see below a commented example of the full producer configuration:"},"6463":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#producer-configuration","content":"Note, when your requirements exceed this configuration, you can still\nuse the underlying low level SDK for fine grained control over every detail of the producer."},"6464":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#iggystreamconsumer-builder","content":"When you have an existing server with multiple producers, you may have to implement a consumer for a specific stream or\ntopic. In some cases you may already have a configured iggy client, in others you may need to create\none. In either case, you can use the IggyStreamConsumer to create the consumer."},"6465":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-consumer-and-client","content":"Similar to the previous StreamBuilder, the IggyConsumerMessageExt is used to process messages using\nthe PrintEventConsumer implementation of the MessageConsumer trait."},"6466":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-consumer-from-an-existing-client","content":"In the event that you have an existing iggy client, you can use the IggyStreamConsumer::build method to create the\nconsumer. To do so, just replace the with_client_from_url with the following:"},"6467":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-33","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-consumer-from-an-existing-client","content":"Notice, you find some utils to build a customized iggy client in\nthe examples folder of the iggy\nrepository."},"6468":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-34","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#consumer-configuration","content":"The default configuration only exists to get you started quickly i.e. to test out an idea, draft an proof of concept or\nsimilar. In practice, you often want fine grained control over the configuration. The IggyConsumerConfig gives you a way\nto configure the consumer in sufficient detail without distracting with low level details.\nPlease note, if you have questions about any of those settings, please ask in\nthe community discord. See below a commented configuration example:"},"6469":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-35","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#consumer-configuration","content":"partitions_count also selects the partition ID for an ordinary consumer. When it creates a topic with n partitions, the valid IDs are 0..n-1, so using the same n as an ordinary consumer's ID fails. The sample uses a consumer group. For an ordinary consumer, create the topic separately and use client.consumer(name, stream, topic, partition_id) to select a valid partition."},"6470":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-36","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#add-consumers-dynamically-at-runtime","content":"When you create consumers on demand at application runtime, the consumer has to survive the window where the stream\nor topic does not exist yet. The init_retries and init_interval settings cover exactly that, and they are enabled\nby default (5 retries with a 3 second interval), so a consumer created shortly before its producer usually just works."},"6471":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-37","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#add-consumers-dynamically-at-runtime","content":"However, when you cannot guarantee that the stream or topic ever gets created by someone else,\nyou have to set create_stream_if_not_exists and create_topic_if_not_exists to true in the IggyConsumerConfig\n(both are false by default) to ensure the consumer creates them and starts correctly. Other than that, you just call\nthe IggyStreamConsumer constructors as before i.e.:"},"6472":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-38","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#add-consumers-dynamically-at-runtime","content":"Here config is the IggyConsumerConfig built above, which selects the stream and topic, initialization retries, and automatic creation behavior."},"6473":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-39","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#add-producers-dynamically-at-runtime","content":"Adding new producers to the iggy server at runtime is as simple as creating a new config and\ncalling the IggyStreamProducer::build method (or with_client_from_url when you need a new client too).\nUnlike the consumer, the producer always creates missing streams or topics by default.\nThe IggyProducerConfig has a convenient constructor to create\na new configuration from just the stream, topic, batch length and linger time. See the example below."},"647