t"],[981,"Committed log position on consensus replies. Direct replies can instead carry the session epoch or zero; this field alone does not prove a commit."],[1794,"Committing an offset before application processing, including poll-time auto-commit, can provide at-most-once processing: a crash can lose unprocessed messages. Committing only after successful processing supports at-least-once processing, with duplicates possible after a crash. Recovery also depends on retention and the configured message and consumer-offset durability. Iggy does not provide an atomic transaction between a consumer offset and an external side effect. Exactly-once application effects require an idempotent operation or an atomic deduplication-and-effect transaction; unique message IDs alone do not provide that guarantee."],[6296,"Committing the offset depending on the particular mode (e.g. in the background based on some interval, when an offset matches the configured trigger etc.)"],[2734,"Common operations"],[4306,"Common Options"],[4426,"Common Options"],[4567,"Common Options"],[4647,"Common Options"],[4955,"Common Options"],[5115,"Common Options"],[5258,"Common Options"],[1776,"Common questions about Apache Iggy, including how it compares to Kafka and other message brokers."],[3764,"Compare the existing policy with madvise if allocator page promotion or compaction is relevant. Direct reclaim and compaction can stall allocations. On recent kernels, per-size policies and explicit MADV_COLLAPSE requests also matter, so the top-level switch alone does not describe all behavior. See transparent huge-page controls."],[3832,"compio + custom binary protocol"],[3836,"compio-quic (quinn)"],[3844,"compio-ws"],[3672,"Completed buffered file writes normally remain in Linux's page cache after\nthe server process dies. Messages still in the process's journal do not.\nPage cache alone does not protect against machine or power failure."],[4106,"Completion policy for explicit consumer-offset stores and deletes, independent of message durability."],[1731,"Component"],[2786,"Component"],[2908,"compose.yml:"],[595,"Compression"],[3987,"Compression"],[491,"Compression codes: 1 = Stored, 2 = Deflated, 3 = Bzip2, 4 = Zstd, 5 = Lzma, 6 = Xz. Snapshot type codes: 1 = FilesystemOverview, 2 = ProcessList, 3 = ResourceUsage, 4 = Test, 5 = ServerLogs, 6 = ServerConfig, 100 = All."],[1290,"Compression is a required positional: none or gzip; it is stored as topic metadata, but compression is not applied. Expiry is a human-readable duration (7d, 1day 12h, unlimited). Omitting expiry at creation selects no expiration."],[5894,"Compression methods: Stored, Deflated, Bzip2, Zstd, Lzma, Xz.\nSnapshot types: FilesystemOverview, ProcessList, ResourceUsage, Test, ServerLogs, ServerConfig, All."],[4091,"compression_algorithm"],[4128,"compression_algorithm"],[5835,"compressionAlgorithm, messageExpiry, and maxTopicSize are optional. messageExpiry is a nullable TimeSpan."],[2327,"Concepts"],[2324,"Concepts explains each of these in more detail."],[3008,"Config file: the path in IGGY_CONFIG_PATH, or core/server/config.toml resolved against the current working directory. A missing file is only a warning. The server continues on embedded defaults."],[4802,"Configurable Payload Storage: Store the payload as raw bytes, native JSONB, or text"],[2169,"Configurable server features (e.g. caching and transport protocols), plus per-topic segment size, durability and flush thresholds"],[83,"Configuration"],[1809,"Configuration"],[2074,"Configuration"],[2965,"Configuration"],[4198,"Configuration"],[4268,"Configuration"],[4305,"Configuration"],[4374,"Configuration"],[4425,"Configuration"],[4566,"Configuration"],[4646,"Configuration"],[4711,"Configuration"],[4790,"Configuration"],[4890,"Configuration"],[4954,"Configuration"],[5036,"Configuration"],[5098,"Configuration"],[5114,"Configuration"],[5201,"Configuration"],[5257,"Configuration"],[5343,"Configuration"],[5476,"Configuration"],[5507,"Configuration"],[6177,"Configuration"],[3715,"Configuration comes from the TOML file named by IGGY_CONFIG_PATH, or core/server/config.toml relative to the working directory. If that file is missing, the server uses the defaults embedded in the binary. Any key can be overridden with an IGGY_-prefixed environment variable, and a .env file in the working directory or its parents (or the file named by IGGY_ENV_PATH) is loaded at startup. See Configuration for the full reference."],[4515,"Configuration example"],[2012,"Configuration Example"],[3006,"Configuration is resolved in three layers. Later layers win:"],[1914,"Configuration Options"],[4516,"Configuration Options"],[5068,"Configuration properties can be overridden using environment variables. Supported scalar fields and indexed stream entries use the pattern: IGGY_CONNECTORS_SINK_[KEY]_[PROPERTY]"],[5540,"Configuration properties can be overridden using environment variables. Supported scalar fields and indexed stream entries use the pattern: IGGY_CONNECTORS_SOURCE_[KEY]_[PROPERTY]"],[1911,"Configuration Providers"],[5049,"Configuration version (u64)."],[5520,"Configuration version (u64)."],[1772,"Configuration: every [cluster] setting and its validation rules"],[6019,"Configure a consumer, initialize it, then iterate. ReceiveAsync returns an IAsyncEnumerable<ReceivedMessage>:"],[5918,"Configure a publisher, initialize it, then send. InitAsync validates (and optionally creates) the stream and topic:"],[5539,"Configure one [[streams]] destination per source instance. The runtime currently retains only the last producer when multiple entries are supplied."],[3806,"Connected clients"],[1380,"Connecting to a server"],[1385,"Connection contexts"],[5666,"Connection events"],[3817,"Connection handling across shards"],[568,"Connection lifecycle"],[1159,"Connection lifecycle: protocol versioning and the login-register handshake."],[4804,"Connection Pooling: Efficient database connection management"],[2647,"Connection receive window size in bytes"],[6066,"Connection semantics"],[5922,"Connection settings - only used when the builder creates its own client (i.e. the Create(streamId, topicId) overload, without an existing client). A builder-created TCP client auto-logs in and reuses the credentials across reconnects. For HTTP, supply an explicitly authenticated client"],[6023,"Connection settings - only used when the builder creates its own client. A builder-created TCP client auto-logs in and reuses the credentials across reconnects. For HTTP, supply an explicitly authenticated client"],[2733,"Connection string"],[2742,"Connection string"],[6286,"Connection string"],[6415,"Connection string"],[2509,"Connection string. A single value such as iggy://user:password@localhost:8090 that tells a client how to connect. The Rust SDK and the SDKs that wrap it (Python, C++ and PHP) accept one. See Connection strings."],[5576,"Connection strings"],[2556,"Connection Strings"],[2568,"Connection strings are supported by the Rust SDK and the SDKs that wrap it (Python, C++, PHP)."],[2813,"Connection strings are supported by the Rust, Python, C++, and PHP SDKs. They provide a compact way to configure the client, including the transport protocol, credentials, server address, and options."],[4813,"connection_string"],[5367,"connection_string"],[4724,"connection_uri"],[5045,"Connector key (String), separate from the numeric plugin instance ID."],[5516,"Connector key (String), separate from the numeric plugin instance ID."],[1976,"Connector runtime has an HTTP API which is enabled by default at 127.0.0.1:8081. It can be disabled by setting the enabled flag to false in the [http] section."],[17,"Connectors"],[1824,"Connectors"],[2145,"Connectors"],[2181,"Connectors - sinks, sources and data transformations based on the custom Rust plugins loaded dynamically at runtime"],[1817,"Connectors are dynamically loaded shared libraries (.so on Linux, .dylib on macOS, .dll on Windows) loaded at runtime via dlopen2. Message batches and metadata crossing the FFI boundary between the runtime and plugins are serialized using postcard, a compact binary format. Plugin configuration is passed as JSON. The SDK initializes one Tokio runtime per loaded plugin library, shared by its connector instances. All plugins run in the same process."],[3600,"Connectors runtime"],[28,"Connectors Runtime - dynamically loaded Rust plugins for data integration: 16 sinks and 4 sources. Ingest from PostgreSQL, Elasticsearch or InfluxDB into Iggy, or forward to MongoDB, Elasticsearch, ClickHouse, Apache Iceberg, Quickwit, S3 and more. Built-in data transforms and Prometheus metrics."],[1737,"Consensus"],[1751,"Consensus group"],[412,"Consensus group (see planes)"],[229,"Consensus planes"],[1513,"Consensus runs on a fixed 10ms tick. Every duration below is converted to whole ticks: values are rounded down to a multiple of 10ms, and anything under 10ms is raised to a single tick rather than firing sooner. repair_gap_debounce_interval has an additional 500ms floor."],[1489,"Consensus timing"],[744,"Consensus view. Send zero."],[4082,"Constraints"],[4513,"Consume messages from Iggy topics and store them in Iceberg tables, with REST catalogs and S3-compatible storage."],[2884,"consume_messages runs until the shutdown event is set. Ctrl-C, or a SIGTERM from a supervisor, sets it, and the current message finishes before the call returns. The instance name is only used in the log."],[588,"Consumer"],[2408,"Consumer"],[5614,"Consumer"],[6071,"Consumer"],[6132,"Consumer"],[6174,"Consumer"],[6253,"Consumer"],[6290,"Consumer"],[2491,"consumer - the type of the consumer (kind + ID), either the default Consumer means the standalone client which does the message polling on its own, independently of the other consumers (unless they would use the same ID), or the ConsumerGroup which might be used to create the group of consumers sharing the common identifier - this is especially useful in the case of the horizontal scaling, where we want to ensure, that the same (and only one) consumer, will poll the messages from the specific partition, and there will be no overlap with the other consumers from the same group. For example, when scaling out (by adding more instances) the group of payment processing microservices, we probably don't want the multiple instances to process the same payment. Group assignment coordinates polling, but application side effects still need idempotency across retries and rebalances."],[3865,"Consumer avg latency: TCP 0.70ms vs WebSocket 1.44ms (+106%)"],[5905,"Consumer builder options"],[6291,"Consumer builder options"],[6441,"Consumer configuration"],[2315,"Consumer group"],[2316,"Consumer group"],[2409,"Consumer group"],[5639,"Consumer group"],[6175,"Consumer group"],[2511,"Consumer group. A set of consumers that share a topic's partitions, with each partition read by one member at a time. See Consumer groups."],[481,"Consumer groups"],[2336,"Consumer groups"],[5617,"Consumer groups"],[5686,"Consumer groups"],[5907,"Consumer groups"],[6211,"Consumer groups"],[90,"Consumer Groups"],[6086,"Consumer groups are assignment-based. JoinConsumerGroupAsync makes this client a member. The assignment is synced on demand and refreshed on every PingAsync. The TCP client pings on its own every HeartbeatInterval (5 seconds by default), so an idle session survives the server's heartbeat verification and assignments stay fresh."],[5887,"Consumer groups coordinate message consumption across multiple consumers, load-balancing partitions between members."],[2177,"Consumer groups distributing partitions across connected clients for horizontal scaling; ordering is per partition, with cooperative partition rebalancing"],[2877,"Consumer groups need a binary transport, so this tutorial uses TCP. HTTP cannot join a group (see Consumer groups and the FAQ)."],[2363,"Consumer groups provide horizontal scaling for message consumption. When multiple consumers join the same consumer group, the server automatically distributes partitions among group members so that each partition has at most one member permitted to poll it within that group. When members join or leave, the server triggers a cooperative partition rebalancing with a pending revocation phase (configurable timeout, default 30s) to let the previous owner finish processing and commit before handoff. Application processing and offset-commit ordering still determine whether failures cause skips or duplicates."],[1822,"Consumer groups track sink offsets automatically. Offsets are committed when messages are polled, before processing by the external sink completes."],[6263,"Consumer groups: await client.consumer_group(...) returns an IggyConsumer that creates and joins the group by default and commits offsets according to the configured AutoCommit mode. Groups require a binary transport; HTTP supports ordinary consumers only."],[6200,"Consumer groups: client.group manages groups (including ensure-and-join in one call), and polling with Consumer.Group(groupId) and partitionId: null follows the partitions assigned to that member."],[3932,"Consumer groups: create, delete, join, leave, get"],[1335,"Consumer name or ID to poll as (default: 0)"],[480,"Consumer offsets"],[91,"Consumer Offsets"],[2353,"Consumer offsets and consumer group offsets"],[5886,"Consumer offsets are keyed by consumer + stream + topic + partition. Note the argument order - the Consumer comes first:"],[6088,"Consumer offsets need an explicit partition. StoreOffsetAsync / DeleteOffsetAsync do not accept a null partition id under VSR. Passing one throws client-side."],[6201,"Consumer streams: singleConsumerStream and groupConsumerStream expose polling as Node streams."],[3634,"consumer_offset_durability"],[4103,"consumer_offset_durability"],[4021,"consumer_offset_durability (default replicated): the independent policy for explicit offset stores and deletes."],[3047,"consumer_offset_durability topic option: replicated or persisted"],[1668,"cons