ts next request. The HTTP session cap tracks this at half. Must be between 2 and 65536."],[5399,"snake_case_columns"],[121,"snapshot"],[1280,"snapshot"],[448,"snapshot.bin verbatim (MessagePack, snapshot format version 5; readers accept versions 3 through 5); frontier = sequence number"],[4039,"snapshot.bin: a MessagePack (rmp-serde) snapshot of the whole metadata state machine, written at checkpoints so the journal can be truncated. Recovery loads the snapshot, then replays journal entries newer than it, rebuilding the client session table alongside."],[5691,"Snapshots"],[2470,"So far, so good, however, before we will be able to publish any messages to our streaming server, at first, we need to create the stream, topic and partition(s) - if you're unfamiliar with these concepts, please refer to concepts where all of them are described in-depth."],[3770,"Socket-buffer requirements depend on bandwidth and round-trip time. For example, 10 Gbit/s across 10 ms has a bandwidth-delay product of about 12.5 MB. If the connection is window-limited, inspect net.core.rmem_max, net.core.wmem_max, net.ipv4.tcp_rmem, and net.ipv4.tcp_wmem. Larger ceilings permit larger buffers, but do not force the application to use them or guarantee higher throughput."],[3656,"Soft segment limit; a whole batch can cross it"],[4098,"Soft size limit per segment: a segment may close one whole batch past it."],[6081,"Some operations are TCP-only and throw FeatureUnavailableException on HTTP: joining/leaving a consumer group, GetMeAsync, DeleteSegmentsAsync, and SendBinaryRequestAsync."],[1732,"Source"],[1812,"Source"],[1825,"Source"],[5504,"Source"],[4521,"Source Compatibility"],[2036,"Source connector config (connectors/protobuf_source.toml):"],[5538,"Source connector config (connectors/random.toml):"],[5510,"Source connectors are responsible for ingesting data from external sources into Apache Iggy. They provide a way to integrate Apache Iggy with various data sources, such as databases, message queues, or file systems."],[2026,"Source connectors can persist their position between restarts. Each batch of ProducedMessages may carry an optional ConnectorState. After processing and sending the batch to Iggy, the runtime saves that state through the configured file or HTTP backend before acknowledging the batch. The last saved state is passed to new() on the next startup. ConnectorState wraps raw bytes and provides serialize/deserialize helpers backed by MessagePack."],[1937,"Source plugins supply optional checkpoint bytes. The default file backend writes them to {state.path}/source_{key}.state using a temporary file, file synchronization and atomic rename. On Unix, it also synchronizes the parent directory. The runtime stores these bytes unchanged; the SDK provides MessagePack serialization helpers."],[2047,"source_format / target_format: Formats to convert between - any schema value (json, raw, text, proto, flat_buffer, avro)"],[1865,"Sources produce messages to an Iggy stream and topic. Configure one [[streams]] entry per source instance: the runtime currently retains only the last configured producer. For example, the Random source connector generates random messages that are then sent to the configured stream and topic."],[4561,"Sources that wrap row data in an envelope need a transform when the table schema describes the inner row. Otherwise, the Arrow JSON reader maps envelope keys to table columns, producing nulls or schema errors."],[2027,"Sources with cursors or destructive operations must stage changes during poll() and apply them on SourceBatchResult::Ack; Nack discards staged progress so the batch can be polled again. The SDK allows one batch in flight and stops polling if on_batch_result() returns an error. Its default no-op is suitable only for sources without staged work."],[1371,"Space-separated subset of filesystem_overview, process_list, resource_usage, test, server_logs, server_config, all"],[1587,"Spanning multiple hosts"],[1142,"StaleClien